#!/usr/bin/env bash
#
# devbin: summary = Sign and notarise the macOS release binaries with the Geodica Developer ID
#
# intent macos <subcommand>  -- run `int macos` with no arguments for the list.
#
# THE SUBCOMMAND LIST LIVES IN usage() AT THE FOOT OF THIS FILE, not here, and it
# moved for a reason worth keeping. The help used to be `sed -n '5,15p'` of this
# header -- a hard-coded line range over a block that grows every time a
# subcommand is added. Adding one pushes the last entry past line 15 and the help
# silently stops listing it: no error, no truncation marker, just a command that
# has stopped being discoverable. **A help text that can go quietly out of date is
# the one place that must not.** One definition, printed and read from the same
# string.
#
# hv, 2026-08-15: "Having Intent properly signed using my Geodica Apple Developer
# Connection keys is the right way to go regardless of whether or not brew needs
# it." So this is not conditional on the Gatekeeper analysis -- ad-hoc happens to
# survive the brew path, and signing properly is the decision anyway.
#
# PORTED FROM LAMPLIGHT (`bin/.devbin/cmd/macos.d/`), which signs the lamplight
# CLI and Wrighter.app with the same Geodica ADC, team 76BQL8L47U. Ported rather
# than reinvented: the credential model, the codesign flags and the notarytool
# flow are all theirs. **This is a fraction of theirs and deliberately one file**
# -- Intent ships two bare Mach-O CLI binaries with no .app bundle, no nested
# executables, no entitlements and no installer pkg, so the inside-out bundle
# walk and the productsign half have no counterpart here. Splitting five short
# subcommands into a `.d/` would only strand the shared credential lookup.
#
# CREDENTIALS LIVE IN THE LOGIN KEYCHAIN AND NOWHERE ELSE. No key material in
# this repository, in CI config, or in any file this writes. `store-creds` is
# interactive and runs once per machine. Nothing here ever prints a secret --
# `env` and `doctor` report PRESENT/MISSING, never a value.
#
# THE THING THAT WILL LOOK LIKE A BUG AND IS NOT: **a bare Mach-O binary cannot
# have a notarisation ticket stapled to it.** `stapler` writes tickets into
# .app/.pkg/.dmg containers; there is nowhere to put one in a bare executable.
# Measured on the closest possible precedent -- conflab, a Developer ID signed
# bare binary shipped through the geodica tap: `stapler validate` says "does not
# have a ticket stapled to it", and that is the correct steady state. The ticket
# lives on Apple's servers and Gatekeeper checks online. Do not "fix" this.
#
# AND `spctl -a -t exec` WILL SAY "rejected" ON A CORRECTLY SIGNED CLI. Measured
# on conflab: `rejected (the code is valid but does not seem to be an app)`, with
# a valid `origin=Developer ID Application: Geodica Pty Ltd`. That is spctl
# refusing to assess a bare executable under a policy meant for app BUNDLES --
# a category error, not a verdict.
#
# **CORRECTION TO THE ABOVE, and it matters because I nearly wrote spctl off.**
# `-t exec` is the wrong policy type, not proof that spctl cannot answer. The
# right invocation for a bare binary is:
#
#   spctl -a -t open --context context:primary-signature -vv <binary>
#
# which on a notarised Intent binary reports `accepted` and, decisively,
# `source=Notarized Developer ID`. **A tool giving a useless answer usually means
# it was asked the wrong question** -- the same shape as probing acceptance with
# `doctor` when the question was acceptance, not health.
#
set -euo pipefail
: "${DEVBIN_LIB:?run me through the devbin dispatcher}"
. "$DEVBIN_LIB/helpers"

ROOT="${PROJECT_ROOT:?}"
RELEASE_DIR="$ROOT/native/rust/target/release"
BINARIES="intent intentd"
KC_PREFIX="intent.apple"

# THE SUPPORT TREE, AND WHY A RELEASE OF TWO BINARIES SHIPS THREE ARTEFACTS.
#
# **A Rust binary cannot carry shell inside itself.** Intent ships its Claude Code
# hooks and its whiteboard guards as FILES under `lib/templates/`, and two shipped
# consumers exec them out of the install tree: `intent claude hook <name>` runs
# `lib/templates/.claude/scripts/<name>.sh`, and the pre-commit gate runs
# `lib/templates/hooks/<guard>.sh` at a path it parses back out of `intent info`.
# The binary finds that tree by walking up from its own symlink-resolved location
# to the directory containing `lib/templates/` (`intentsvcs::install::MARKER`),
# with no environment fallback -- deliberately, because a stale v2 `$INTENT_HOME`
# would otherwise make a v3 binary exec v2's hook scripts.
#
# A formula that installs only the binaries therefore produces an install that
# cannot find itself. Measured against a reproduction of exactly what the formula
# builds -- binary under `Cellar/intent/3.0.0/bin/`, brew-style symlink, neutral
# cwd (probe at 501f5083): `intent info` and `intent claude hook` both fail, and
# the gate's guard path comes back unresolvable. **Every consumer project would
# silently lose its session hooks and both whiteboard guards.**
#
# THE TARBALL'S ROOT IS THE INSTALL ROOT, one level under a wrapper directory,
# and that is the one design decision here worth defending. **The wrapper is not
# decoration -- see SUPPORT_PREFIX. Without it this archive installs to the wrong
# depth and the install cannot find itself, which is the exact failure the
# paragraph above says the archive exists to prevent. Measured on the first real
# brew install, 2026-08-26.** It contains `lib/templates/...`
# rather than `templates/...`, so the formula's install line is "put everything in
# this archive into libexec" rather than "put templates in libexec/lib". Two
# unimplemented commands -- `intent critic` and `intent claude rules`, both exit 2
# in this build -- will need the rule library, which lives OUTSIDE the marker
# directory (`intent/plugins/claude/rules/`). When they land, the shipped set
# grows. Rooting the archive at the install root means that is a CONTENT change,
# not a formula change and not a second asset.
#
# IT IS NOT SIGNED AND NOT NOTARISED, AND THAT IS NOT AN OMISSION. Notarisation
# assesses Mach-O binaries; a gzipped tar of shell and markdown has nothing to
# sign and nowhere to hold a ticket. The control that protects it is the same one
# that protects the binaries: `publish` re-downloads what it uploaded and hashes
# THAT. What this DOES require is that `checksum` stop treating "staged artefact"
# and "must be proven notarised" as the same set -- see cmd_checksum, where an
# unclassified artefact is now a refusal rather than a default.
SUPPORT_ASSET="intent-support.tar.gz"

# THE ONE HOME for what the support tree carries. Relative to $ROOT. Every path
# v3 resolves against its install root is either here or comes from the binaries
# themselves, and `support_paths_coverage` below holds that sentence to the
# source rather than to this comment.
#
# THIS LIST WAS `lib/templates` ALONE UNTIL 2026-08-26, AND THE COMMENT THAT KEPT
# IT THAT WAY WAS NOT LAZY -- IT WAS VERIFIED AGAINST THE WRONG POPULATION. It
# read: "everything the shipped consumers reach into is under it -- verified
# rather than assumed: the only path any shipped SCRIPT references outside its
# own tree is `$INTENT_HOME_RESOLVED`". That verification was real and correct
# for shipped scripts. The rules and skills trees are read by the RUST BINARY,
# which was never in the population -- so the check could not have failed, and
# the words `verified rather than assumed` are exactly why nobody looked again.
#
# What it cost: the 3.0.0 keg shipped no rule library and no skills tree, so on
# every project running it `intent critic <lang> --staged` armed 0 of 0 rules,
# printed `ok` and exited 0, and the pre-commit hook branched on that rc
# correctly and passed. Found by devbin/vc as a PACKAGING gap rather than a
# resolver fault, which is the call that pointed here instead of into the rules
# loader; located to this line by vc; resolver enumerated by dc.
#
# A RECORDED VERIFICATION MUST NAME ITS POPULATION, or the next reader inherits
# the confidence without the scope.
SUPPORT_PATHS="lib/templates intent/plugins/claude/rules intent/plugins/claude/skills"

# THE WRAPPER DIRECTORY, AND IT EXISTS BECAUSE HOMEBREW UNPACKS WITH A HEURISTIC.
#
# `resource.stage` STRIPS A SINGLE TOP-LEVEL DIRECTORY before running the install
# block -- the standard "unwrap the release tarball" behaviour, applied when the
# unpacked archive has exactly ONE top-level entry and it is a directory.
#
# MEASURED, NOT READ: rooted at `lib/templates/...` this archive's only top-level
# entry was `lib`. Brew stripped it, staged `templates/`, and the tree landed at
# `libexec/templates` -- one level short of the `lib/templates` marker that
# `install::home` walks for. Every file shipped correctly and NOTHING could reach
# them: `intent --version` answered perfectly, `intent info` printed
# `INTENT_HOME: <not set>`, and every hook refused at exit 1. **rc=1 is the code
# Claude Code does not block on and the code eleven vendored devbin estates fail
# OPEN on, so a fleet on that install loses every session hook and five guards per
# estate with every commit still succeeding.**
#
# The wrapper makes the strip DETERMINISTIC INSTEAD OF INCIDENTAL. There is always
# exactly one top-level entry, brew always removes exactly the wrapper, and what
# is staged is always the install root.
#
# WHY NOT THE ONE-LINE FORMULA FIX. `(libexec/"lib").install Dir["*"]` corrects
# today's layout and is wrong for the change already planned: when `intent critic`
# and `intent claude rules` land they need the rule library at
# `intent/plugins/claude/rules/`, OUTSIDE this marker directory, and that second
# tree would land under `libexec/lib/intent/`. The fragile fix and the robust fix
# are the same size; only one survives growth. The formula line stays
# "install whatever the archive carries".
SUPPORT_PREFIX="intent-support"

# WHERE THE ARTEFACTS GO AND WHERE THE CLAIM ABOUT THEM GOES. Two repositories,
# by hv's ruling of 2026-08-15 (D40): artefacts live on the source repo's own
# releases, and the tap carries only the formula that names them.
#
# The three spellings are all real and none is derivable from the others without
# a rule someone has to remember: GitHub calls the tap `homebrew-intent`, brew
# calls the same thing `matthewsinclair/intent`, and the source repo is a third
# name again. Written out once rather than reconstructed at each use.
REPO="matthewsinclair/intent"
TAP="matthewsinclair/homebrew-intent"
BREW_TAP="matthewsinclair/intent"

# MEASURED 2026-08-15: neither `intent` nor `intentd` exists in homebrew-core, so
# the names are free and `brew install intent` would resolve to our tap once it
# is tapped. **We still print the fully-qualified `matthewsinclair/intent/intent`
# everywhere, deliberately.** The short form works only while core stays empty of
# that name, and the day core gains one, every published instruction becomes
# ambiguous at best and installs somebody else's software at worst -- in an
# instruction users have already copied into their own docs. The long form cannot
# be shadowed and costs one line of typing.

# EVERYTHING AFTER THE COPY HAPPENS HERE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT.
#
# `target/release/` is SHARED MUTABLE STATE. Four sessions build in this tree and
# `cargo build --release` is the most ordinary thing any of them does -- so a
# signature living there is not a property of a file, it is a property of a file
# that nobody has rebuilt yet.
#
# MEASURED LIVE, 2026-08-15: a peer's release build replaced a Developer ID
# signature with the linker's ad-hoc one, and one of two shipped binaries stopped
# being notarised inside the hour, with NO signal and every artefact of the
# earlier proof still reading as valid. Nobody did anything wrong; a shared target
# directory is what it is for.
#
# The first response was to make `stage` REFUSE to checksum anything it could not
# prove. That was right and it stays -- but a refusal DETECTS the race, it does
# not remove it, and it detects it only at the end, after a notarisation round
# trip has already been spent on bytes that no longer exist.
#
# So the target moved instead. `target/dist/` is written by this command and by
# nothing else, which means the window in which a peer can disturb an artefact
# shrinks from "the whole sign -> notarise -> stage sequence", minutes long and
# spanning a network round trip to Apple, to "the ditto on the line below". **A
# structure that cannot fail beats a control that catches the failure**, and the
# control is still there underneath because catching it is free.
STAGE_DIR="$ROOT/native/rust/target/dist"

# WHAT COMMIT DID THESE BYTES COME FROM. Written by `stage`, read by `publish`.
#
# A SIBLING of STAGE_DIR rather than a file inside it, for two reasons that both
# had to hold. `stage` opens with `rm -rf "$STAGE_DIR"`, so a record inside it
# would be destroyed and rewritten by the very step whose inputs it describes --
# harmless, but it makes the record's lifetime identical to the staging
# directory's, which is exactly when you want to be able to compare them. And
# `unclassified_artefacts` REFUSES any staged file that is not a proven binary or
# the declared support asset, so a provenance file inside STAGE_DIR would be
# rejected by the guard added beside it -- correctly, since it is not something
# that ships.
PROVENANCE_FILE="$ROOT/native/rust/target/dist-provenance.txt"

# The triple comes from `rustc -vV`, never from `uname`: the artefact name is a
# claim about what the compiler targeted, and only the compiler can answer that.
host_triple() {
  local t
  t="$(rustc -vV | sed -n 's/^host: //p')"
  [ -n "$t" ] || die "could not read the host target triple from 'rustc -vV'"
  printf '%s' "$t"
}

# --- credentials -------------------------------------------------------------
# Read from the login Keychain, never from the environment or a file. Silent on
# a miss; the callers report presence.
kc_get() { security find-generic-password -a "$USER" -s "$KC_PREFIX.$1" -w 2>/dev/null || true; }

APPLE_ID="$(kc_get APPLE_ID)"
APPLE_APP_PASSWORD="$(kc_get APPLE_APP_PASSWORD)"
APPLE_TEAM_ID="$(kc_get APPLE_TEAM_ID)"

# The identity is DERIVED from the team id rather than stored, so there is one
# place a team id can be wrong. Falls back to discovering it from the keychain
# when no team id is stored yet -- which is the normal state before store-creds,
# and is what lets `sign` work today while notarisation waits on hv.
if [ -n "$APPLE_TEAM_ID" ]; then
  IDENTITY="Developer ID Application: Geodica Pty Ltd ($APPLE_TEAM_ID)"
else
  IDENTITY="$(security find-identity -v -p codesigning 2>/dev/null |
    sed -n 's/.*"\(Developer ID Application: [^"]*\)".*/\1/p' | head -1)"
fi

present() { [ -n "$1" ] && printf 'PRESENT' || printf 'MISSING'; }

require_identity() {
  [ -n "$IDENTITY" ] || die "no Developer ID Application identity in the keychain -- run: int macos doctor"
}

require_binaries() {
  local missing=""
  for b in $BINARIES; do
    [ -f "$RELEASE_DIR/$b" ] || missing="$missing $b"
  done
  [ -z "$missing" ] || die "missing release binaries:$missing -- run 'int build all' first (it already defaults to --release; passing it again is refused by cargo)"
}

# require_staged -- every subcommand after `stage` acts on the staged copies, so
# each one has to be able to say WHICH step has not been run rather than failing
# on a missing path. Names the fix, not the symptom.
require_staged() {
  local triple missing=""
  triple="$(host_triple)"
  for b in $BINARIES; do
    [ -f "$STAGE_DIR/$b-$triple" ] || missing="$missing $b-$triple"
  done
  [ -f "$STAGE_DIR/$SUPPORT_ASSET" ] || missing="$missing $SUPPORT_ASSET"
  [ -z "$missing" ] || die "nothing staged:$missing -- run 'int macos stage' first (it copies the build output into $STAGE_DIR, where a peer's cargo build cannot reach it)"
}

# is_binary_artefact <file> <triple> -- is this staged file one of the Mach-O
# artefacts, and therefore something that MUST be proven signed and notarised
# before anything hashes it?
#
# Derived from $BINARIES rather than matched against a pattern. The staged names
# used to be identified by the glob `*-$triple`, which answers a question about
# SPELLING when the question is about KIND -- and a data artefact that happened to
# end in the triple would have been fed to `verify_notarised`, while one that did
# not would have been silently left out of SHA256SUMS.txt. Neither failure names
# itself.
is_binary_artefact() {
  local f="$1" triple="$2" b
  for b in $BINARIES; do
    [ "$f" = "$b-$triple" ] && return 0
  done
  return 1
}

# staged_artefacts -- everything in the staging directory that is a candidate for
# publication. SHA256SUMS.txt is the claim ABOUT these files and never one of
# them, so it is the single exclusion.
staged_artefacts() {
  [ -d "$STAGE_DIR" ] || return 0
  (cd "$STAGE_DIR" && ls -1) | grep -v '^SHA256SUMS\.txt$' || true
}

# unclassified_artefacts -- staged files that are neither a Mach-O we must prove
# nor a data asset we have decided to ship unproven.
#
# EXTRACTED SO IT CAN BE DRIVEN. Inline in cmd_checksum this was reachable only
# through a full release run -- stage, sign, and a multi-minute notarisation
# round trip to Apple -- which is another way of saying it was never going to be
# tested and its first exercise would have been a release. The refusal is the
# part worth proving; proving it should not cost a trip to Apple.
unclassified_artefacts() {
  local triple="$1" f out=""
  for f in $(staged_artefacts); do
    is_binary_artefact "$f" "$triple" && continue
    [ "$f" = "$SUPPORT_ASSET" ] && continue
    out="$out $f"
  done
  printf '%s' "$out"
}

# support_tree_drift -- how the built archive differs from the source tree, in
# both directions. Empty output means they agree.
#
# `comm -3` over two sorted lists rather than a per-file grep, so the answer is
# derived from the sets rather than from a list of names someone maintained.
# .DS_Store is excluded on BOTH sides, because it is excluded when the archive is
# built and comparing an exclusion against a non-exclusion reports drift that is
# not drift.
support_tree_drift() {
  local archive="$STAGE_DIR/$SUPPORT_ASSET"
  [ -f "$archive" ] || { printf 'no archive at %s' "$archive"; return 0; }
  comm -3 \
    <(cd "$ROOT" && find $SUPPORT_PATHS -type f ! -name '.DS_Store' | sort) \
    <(tar -tzf "$archive" | grep -v '/$' | grep -v '\.DS_Store$' |
      sed "s#^$SUPPORT_PREFIX/##" | sort) |
    sed -e 's/^\t/       > /' -e 's/^\([^ \t]\)/       < \1/'
}

# What the BINARIES put in the install root, as opposed to the support tarball.
# The formula installs these directly, so the copy list must not be expected to
# carry them and the coverage check below must not demand it.
INSTALL_BINARY_PATHS="bin"

# support_paths_coverage -- paths v3 resolves against its install root that
# SUPPORT_PATHS does not ship. Empty output means the copy list covers its
# consumer.
#
# THE CHECK ONE LEVEL UP FROM `support_tree_drift`, AND THE LEVEL THE 3.0.0
# DEFECT LIVED AT. That one asks "does the ARCHIVE match the COPY LIST", and it
# was green and correct throughout while the copy list was missing two of the
# three trees the binary resolves. Nothing compared the copy list to the thing
# that reads it, so the archive faithfully carried an incomplete answer.
#
# POPULATION, NAMED, BECAUSE NAMING IT IS THE WHOLE LESSON OF THE DEFECT: `src/`
# only. A `tests/` join builds a FIXTURE install root -- `skills_sync.rs` mkdirs
# two of these paths in a tempdir -- so a fixture path is not a shipping
# requirement, and reading tests here would refuse a release over a temp dir.
#
# COVERED means a path-boundary prefix in EITHER direction, and both directions
# are load-bearing. An entry that is a PARENT of the literal ships it, so
# `lib/templates` covers `canon.rs`'s `lib/templates/hooks/pre-commit.sh`. A
# literal that is a parent of an ENTRY exists because its children ship, so
# `plugins.rs`'s `intent/plugins` needs nothing of its own. A new sibling tree
# is neither, which is exactly the case this exists to catch.
support_paths_coverage() {
  local src="$ROOT/native/rust/crates" lit entry covered out=""
  [ -d "$src" ] || { printf '       < no rust source at %s\n' "$src"; return 0; }
  # THE WHOLE CHAIN, NOT THE FIRST LINK. `plugins.rs` writes
  # `install.join("intent").join("plugins")`, and a pattern that stops at the
  # first `.join(...)` reads that as `intent` -- which is covered by any entry
  # under `intent/`, so a chained NEW tree would pass while its first segment
  # carried it. Found by driving the positive control below, not by reading.
  for lit in $(find "$src" -path '*/src/*' -name '*.rs' -exec \
      grep -hoE '(install|install_root|home)(\.join\("[^"]+"\))+' {} + 2>/dev/null |
      sed -E -e 's/^(install|install_root|home)//' -e 's/\.join\("/\//g' \
             -e 's/"\)//g' -e 's#^/##' | sort -u); do
    covered=""
    for entry in $SUPPORT_PATHS $INSTALL_BINARY_PATHS; do
      case "$lit/" in "$entry"/*) covered=y ;; esac
      case "$entry/" in "$lit"/*) covered=y ;; esac
    done
    [ -n "$covered" ] || out="$out       < $lit
"
  done
  printf '%s' "$out"
}

# provenance_blockers <root> -- the reasons this tree cannot produce a release
# that names its own source, one per line. Empty output means it can.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS, AND IT IS A HOLE IN THIS FILE THAT NOTHING WAS WATCHING.
# `publish` already refuses a dev version, refuses to overwrite published bytes,
# and refuses when the tag does not exist -- so it establishes that a tag IS
# there, and then publishes bytes with no evidence they came from it. Two
# independent ways they might not have:
#
#   1. `stage` COPIES the binaries out of `native/rust/target/release`. It does
#      not build them. That directory is shared mutable state in a clone several
#      sessions work in, so the bytes staged can be a peer's build of their own
#      uncommitted work -- measured on this estate twice, once with a rebuild
#      that silently compiled another node's in-flight edit.
#   2. The support tarball is built with `tar -C "$ROOT"` straight from the
#      WORKING TREE, so uncommitted shell ships verbatim. That one arrived with
#      the support asset itself and is the sharper of the two, because it needs
#      no peer and no stale cache -- an editor with unsaved-but-written changes
#      is enough.
#
# The round-trip check in `publish` cannot see either. It re-downloads what it
# uploaded and hashes THAT, which proves the pipeline is internally consistent
# and says nothing about correspondence to the tag it publishes under. **Every
# check here was asking "do these bytes agree with each other", and none was
# asking "are these the bytes the tag names".**
#
# Pure and root-parameterised so it can be driven against fixture repositories.
# The control that matters is the negative one -- a clean checkout must return
# NOTHING -- because a blocker function that fires on everything reads exactly
# like a blocker function that works.
provenance_blockers() {
  local root="$1" dirt
  if ! git -C "$root" rev-parse --git-dir >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    printf 'not a git checkout, so nothing staged here can name a commit\n'
    return 0
  fi
  # Narrow first, because this one is certain: these paths are archived from the
  # working tree, so dirt here is not a risk of shipping uncommitted bytes, it IS
  # shipping them.
  dirt="$(git -C "$root" status --porcelain -- $SUPPORT_PATHS 2>/dev/null)"
  [ -z "$dirt" ] ||
    printf 'the support tree is dirty and is archived VERBATIM from the working tree, so these WOULD ship:\n%s\n' "$dirt"
  # Then the wide one, which is weaker evidence and still worth refusing on: a
  # dirty tree anywhere means the release build may carry changes no commit has.
  dirt="$(git -C "$root" status --porcelain 2>/dev/null)"
  [ -z "$dirt" ] ||
    printf 'the working tree is dirty, so target/release may hold bytes that match no commit\n'
}

# artefact_source_commit MOVED to `cmd/artefact.lib` 2026-08-21 (dc), sourced just
# below. Its three call sites here are unchanged; the move is what keeps them the
# only copy now that `cmd/local` needs the same reader. It went to `lib/helpers`
# first and that was WRONG -- that file is devbin-owned and checksummed, so the
# next `devbin upgrade` would have deleted the function out from under these three.
. "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")/shared/artefact.lib"

# artefact_commit_blockers <stage-dir> <triple> <want-commit> -- reasons the
# STAGED BYTES cannot be shown to have come from <want-commit>.
#
# THE GAP THIS CLOSES, AND IT IS STRUCTURAL RATHER THAN AN OVERSIGHT IN ANY ONE
# CHECK. `stage` writes `commit: $(git rev-parse HEAD)`. `publish` refuses unless
# `traceable: yes` and unless `prov_commit == tag_commit`. EVERY ONE OF THOSE IS
# A CLAIM ABOUT THE CHECKOUT, and until this function existed nothing in the
# pipeline compared the BINARIES to anything at all. So a clean checkout, at the
# tag, with `target/release` holding bytes built forty-two hours earlier
# satisfied every gate -- measured on this estate, `intent` three hours older
# than the commit it was recorded under and `intentd` forty-two, and forty-two
# hours apart from each other. Two trees wearing one sha (cc's phrase).
#
# It is the same shape as the round-trip check one level up: a set of controls
# that all take their inputs from the same place cannot disagree, so their
# agreement carries no information. THE QUESTION NONE OF THEM ASKED IS WHETHER
# THESE ARE THE BYTES THE TAG NAMES, and only the artefact can answer it.
#
# COMPARED AGAINST THE TAG, NOT HEAD, for the reason publish already gives about
# `prov_commit`: publishing is allowed to happen after the branch has moved on,
# and what matters is correspondence to the thing being published rather than to
# the thing being worked on. A stage-time comparison against HEAD would also be
# a comparison between two things the checkout supplied, which is the class of
# check AC-11.5 exists to reject.
#
# PURE AND PARAMETERISED SO IT CAN BE DRIVEN, following this file's own precedent
# and for its stated reason: inline in `cmd_publish` this would be reachable only
# through a full release -- stage, sign, and a multi-minute notarisation round
# trip -- which is another way of saying its first exercise would have been a
# release. The control that matters is the NEGATIVE one: staged binaries built
# at the tag must return NOTHING, because a blocker function that fires on
# everything reads exactly like a blocker function that works.
#
# A RELEASE SHIPS THREE ARTEFACTS AND THIS EXAMINES TWO, WHICH IS A JUSTIFIED
# ASYMMETRY AND NOT A GAP -- WRITTEN DOWN BECAUSE THE NEXT READER WILL SPOT THE
# COUNT AND EITHER ADD A BOGUS CHECK OR ASSUME A HOLE. The two binaries are
# COPIED here out of a build directory, so the only thing that knows what built
# them is the artefact, and nothing in the checkout can answer for them. The
# support tarball is ARCHIVED from the working tree by `tar -C "$ROOT"`, so its
# provenance question is genuinely a question about the CHECKOUT -- "was the tree
# clean when this was rolled up" -- and `provenance_blockers` answers it with a
# NARROW `git status --porcelain -- $SUPPORT_PATHS`, which is the sharper of its
# two arms precisely because dirt there is not a risk of shipping uncommitted
# bytes, it IS shipping them. DIFFERENT ARTEFACTS, DIFFERENT SUBJECTS, DIFFERENT
# CORRECT CHECKS. Adding a marker to a tarball of shell would be a check whose
# subject does not exist; leaving the tarball unexamined would be the compound
# artefact problem this function was built to fix. It is neither.
#
# BOTH BINARIES CARRY THE EMBED (`4c839def`), INCLUDING `intentd`. An earlier
# draft of this comment said `intentd` had none and that a refused publish was
# the deliberate fail-closed state; that was true for about an hour and is
# corrected here rather than deleted, because the reasoning still governs the
# next binary anyone adds to $BINARIES: a check that PASSED an artefact which
# stayed silent would be the vacuous green this whole row is about, so a new
# binary without the embed must refuse rather than be exempted.
#
# What still fails closed, correctly, is a STALE artefact: there is no
# `rerun-if-changed` on `.git/HEAD` (that trigger means "a peer committed" in a
# five-session clone), so a HEAD move outside a package leaves its embed naming
# an earlier commit -- measured -- and that is exactly the disagreement this
# refuses on. A `cargo build` at the tag is the remedy, and it is one command.
artefact_commit_blockers() {
  local dir="$1" triple="$2" want="$3" b bin embedded
  for b in $BINARIES; do
    bin="$dir/$b-$triple"
    if [ ! -f "$bin" ]; then
      printf '%s is not staged, so it cannot be asked what it was built from\n' "$b-$triple"
      continue
    fi
    if ! embedded="$(artefact_source_commit "$bin")"; then
      printf '%s carries NO source-commit marker -- its bytes name no commit at all, so nothing here can attach them to one\n' "$b-$triple"
      continue
    fi
    if [ "$embedded" = "unknown" ]; then
      printf '%s says its source commit is UNKNOWN -- built where git could not answer\n' "$b-$triple"
    elif [ "${embedded#dirty-}" != "$embedded" ]; then
      printf '%s was built from an UNCOMMITTED tree (%s) -- its bytes match no commit, so they match no tag\n' "$b-$triple" "$embedded"
    elif [ "$embedded" != "$want" ]; then
      printf '%s was built at %s, which is not %s -- these are bytes from an earlier tree\n' "$b-$triple" "$embedded" "$want"
    fi
  done
}

# --- subcommands -------------------------------------------------------------

cmd_env() {
  printf 'identity:   %s\n' "${IDENTITY:-<none found>}"
  printf 'team id:    %s\n' "${APPLE_TEAM_ID:-<not stored>}"
  printf '\nnotary credentials (values are never printed):\n'
  printf '  APPLE_ID            %s\n' "$(present "$APPLE_ID")"
  printf '  APPLE_APP_PASSWORD  %s\n' "$(present "$APPLE_APP_PASSWORD")"
  printf '  APPLE_TEAM_ID       %s\n' "$(present "$APPLE_TEAM_ID")"
}

cmd_doctor() {
  local rc=0

  step "Developer ID identity"
  if [ -n "$IDENTITY" ]; then
    printf '    %s\n' "$IDENTITY"
  else
    printf '    MISSING -- no "Developer ID Application" in the login keychain\n'
    rc=1
  fi

  # A REAL test-sign, not a presence check. An identity can be listed and still
  # fail to sign -- expired, revoked, or missing its private key -- and finding
  # that out during a release is finding it out too late.
  step "test-sign against a throwaway binary"
  if [ -n "$IDENTITY" ]; then
    local tmp
    tmp="$(mktemp -d)"
    cp /bin/echo "$tmp/probe" 2>/dev/null || true
    if [ -f "$tmp/probe" ] &&
      codesign --force --options runtime --timestamp --sign "$IDENTITY" "$tmp/probe" >/dev/null 2>&1 &&
      codesign --verify --strict "$tmp/probe" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
      printf '    signs and verifies\n'
    else
      printf '    FAILED -- the identity is listed but cannot produce a valid signature\n'
      rc=1
    fi
    rm -rf "$tmp"
  else
    printf '    skipped (no identity)\n'
  fi

  step "notary credentials in the login keychain"
  printf '    APPLE_ID %s   APPLE_APP_PASSWORD %s   APPLE_TEAM_ID %s\n' \
    "$(present "$APPLE_ID")" "$(present "$APPLE_APP_PASSWORD")" "$(present "$APPLE_TEAM_ID")"
  if [ -z "$APPLE_ID" ] || [ -z "$APPLE_APP_PASSWORD" ] || [ -z "$APPLE_TEAM_ID" ]; then
    printf '    notarisation is UNAVAILABLE until these exist -- run: int macos store-creds\n'
    printf '    (signing does NOT need them and works now)\n'
  fi

  # REPORT THE TEAM, NOT THE `Signature=` LINE, and the first version of this got
  # it backwards in the worst available direction. `codesign -dv` prints
  # `Signature=adhoc` for an ad-hoc binary but `Signature size=NNNN` for a real
  # one -- a SPACE, not an `=`. So `sed -n 's/^Signature=//p'` matched an ad-hoc
  # binary and printed NOTHING for a correctly Developer ID signed one: **the
  # display went blank exactly when the news was good.** hv ran `doctor` after
  # signing and saw two empty fields.
  #
  # Nothing-means-bad is the same false-report shape as an unwired guard, and it
  # is worse in a doctor, whose entire job is to be believed about state. The
  # team identifier is the honest discriminator: absent on ad-hoc, present and
  # named on Developer ID.
  step "release binaries"
  for b in $BINARIES; do
    if [ ! -f "$RELEASE_DIR/$b" ]; then
      printf '    %-8s not built\n' "$b"
      continue
    fi
    local desc team
    team="$(codesign -dv "$RELEASE_DIR/$b" 2>&1 | sed -n 's/^TeamIdentifier=//p')"
    case "$team" in
      "" ) desc="UNSIGNED" ;;
      "not set" ) desc="ad-hoc (linker) -- not Developer ID; run: int macos sign" ;;
      * ) desc="Developer ID ($team)" ;;
    esac
    printf '    %-8s %s\n' "$b" "$desc"
  done

  return $rc
}

cmd_sign() {
  require_identity
  require_staged
  local triple flags
  triple="$(host_triple)"
  printf 'identity: %s\n\n' "$IDENTITY"
  for b in $BINARIES; do
    step "signing $b-$triple"
    # --options runtime  hardened runtime; notarisation REFUSES without it
    # --timestamp        secure timestamp; notarisation REFUSES without it
    # --force            replace the ad-hoc signature the linker already applied
    #                    (arm64 binaries are always signed; this is a re-sign)
    codesign --force --options runtime --timestamp --sign "$IDENTITY" "$STAGE_DIR/$b-$triple"
    # --strict, and READ WHAT THIS PROVES: that the signature present is internally
    # valid. It is NOT evidence the binary is ours -- `codesign --verify --strict`
    # returns 0 on the linker's ad-hoc signature too, because an ad-hoc signature
    # is a valid signature. This board carried it as "the check that means
    # something" for hours and it took a live incident to refute. The check that
    # means something is `verify_notarised`, on a quarantined copy.
    codesign --verify --strict --verbose=2 "$STAGE_DIR/$b-$triple" 2>&1 | sed 's/^/    /'
    # READ THE HARDENED RUNTIME BACK OFF THE ARTEFACT. `--options runtime` above
    # is a REQUEST; whether the flag is actually set is a property of the bytes,
    # and the summary below used to assert it from the request alone. Measured
    # both directions on real binaries before this was written: a binary signed
    # without it reports `flags=0x0(none)`, with it `flags=0x10000(runtime)`, and
    # an untouched Rust release binary `flags=0x20002(adhoc,linker-signed)`.
    #
    # Notarisation refuses without it, so an unmeasured claim here is discovered
    # minutes later at Apple -- after this pipeline has reported success twice.
    flags="$(codesign -d --verbose=2 "$STAGE_DIR/$b-$triple" 2>&1 |
      sed -n 's/.*flags=\([^ ]*\).*/\1/p' | head -1)"
    case "$flags" in
      *runtime*) printf '    %-34s %s\n' "hardened runtime" "$flags" ;;
      *) die "$b-$triple was signed but the hardened runtime is NOT set on it (flags=${flags:-<unreadable>}).
       codesign accepted --options runtime and the artefact does not carry it, so
       notarisation would refuse these bytes. Nothing further should hash or
       publish them." ;;
    esac
  done
  printf '\n'
  ok
  printf '  Both STAGED binaries carry a Developer ID signature, and the hardened\n'
  printf '  runtime flag was read back off each one rather than assumed from the\n'
  printf '  request. Signing rewrote their bytes, so any earlier checksum is stale.\n'
  printf '  NEXT: int macos notarize   (needs the notary credentials)\n'
}

cmd_notarize() {
  require_identity
  require_staged
  local triple
  triple="$(host_triple)"
  [ -n "$APPLE_ID" ] && [ -n "$APPLE_APP_PASSWORD" ] && [ -n "$APPLE_TEAM_ID" ] ||
    die "notary credentials missing -- run: int macos store-creds (see: int macos doctor)"

  # Refuse to submit an unsigned or ad-hoc binary. notarytool would reject it
  # anyway, minutes later, with a less useful message -- and a gate that fails
  # early beats one that fails after a round trip to Apple.
  for b in $BINARIES; do
    codesign -dv "$STAGE_DIR/$b-$triple" 2>&1 | grep -q 'TeamIdentifier=not set' &&
      die "$b-$triple is not Developer ID signed -- run 'int macos sign' first"
  done

  local staging payload zip
  staging="$(mktemp -d)"
  payload="$staging/intent-macos"
  zip="$staging/intent-macos.zip"
  mkdir -p "$payload"

  # ditto throughout, never cp or zip(1): ditto preserves the code signature and
  # the extended attributes notarisation reads, and a plain zip can strip them.
  #
  # STAGE INTO A DIRECTORY FIRST. `ditto -c -k` takes exactly ONE source, so
  # passing both binaries fails with "Can't archive multiple sources" -- which is
  # what the first version of this did. Notarytool is happy with a zip of a
  # directory; it walks it and assesses every Mach-O inside.
  for b in $BINARIES; do
    /usr/bin/ditto "$STAGE_DIR/$b-$triple" "$payload/$b-$triple" || die "could not stage $b-$triple"
  done
  /usr/bin/ditto -c -k --keepParent --sequesterRsrc "$payload" "$zip" ||
    die "could not build the submission archive"

  step "submitting to Apple (typically 1-5 minutes; notarytool waits)"
  # `|| true` deliberately: notarytool exits non-zero on an Invalid verdict, and
  # the verdict is what we want to read and report rather than abort over.
  local out status
  out="$(xcrun notarytool submit "$zip" \
    --apple-id "$APPLE_ID" --password "$APPLE_APP_PASSWORD" --team-id "$APPLE_TEAM_ID" \
    --wait 2>&1)" || true
  printf '%s\n' "$out"
  status="$(printf '%s' "$out" | awk '/^  status: / { print $2 }' | tail -1)"

  if [ "$status" != "Accepted" ]; then
    local id
    id="$(printf '%s' "$out" | awk '/id: [0-9a-f-]{36}/ { print $2; exit }')"
    [ -n "$id" ] && xcrun notarytool log "$id" \
      --apple-id "$APPLE_ID" --password "$APPLE_APP_PASSWORD" --team-id "$APPLE_TEAM_ID" 2>&1 | head -40
    rm -rf "$staging"
    die "notarisation was not Accepted (status: ${status:-unknown})"
  fi

  rm -rf "$staging"
  printf '\n'
  ok
  # WAIT FOR THE TICKET TO BECOME VISIBLE, BECAUSE `Accepted` AND `USABLE` ARE
  # DIFFERENT FACTS AND THIS COMMAND USED TO RETURN ON THE FIRST ONE.
  #
  # MEASURED, not anticipated. A submission came back Accepted with both binaries
  # signed and hardened-runtime-verified, and Gatekeeper then rejected one of them
  # as `Unnotarized Developer ID` for over two minutes -- six polls, both copy
  # methods agreeing at every moment, so it was the ticket and not the check. It
  # then flipped to `Notarized` and stayed, for both binaries. Latency has a
  # direction; that shape is what distinguishes it from a per-artefact gap.
  #
  # WHY IT MATTERS RATHER THAN BEING A CURIOSITY: `prepare` runs `checksum`
  # seconds after this returns, and `checksum` refuses on `verify_notarised`. So
  # the one-pass command was reaching into this window and refusing with "a staged
  # artefact is not signed AND notarised" -- naming the artefact for a fact about
  # a CDN. A refusal that names the wrong subject, in a pipeline being corrected
  # for exactly that.
  #
  # THIS REPORTS AND DOES NOT REFUSE. The authoritative refusal stays in
  # `checksum`, on the one `verify_notarised` implementation; putting a second one
  # here would be the drift AC-11.4 forbids. All this does is stop asking the
  # question during the interval when the answer is known to be meaningless.
  step "waiting for the ticket to become visible to Gatekeeper"
  local waited=0 visible=0 vb
  while [ "$waited" -lt 300 ]; do
    visible=1
    for vb in $BINARIES; do
      verify_notarised "$vb-$triple" >/dev/null 2>&1 || visible=0
    done
    [ "$visible" -eq 1 ] && break
    sleep 15
    waited=$((waited + 15))
  done
  if [ "$visible" -eq 1 ]; then
    printf '    %-34s after %ss\n' "visible to Gatekeeper" "$waited"
  else
    printf '    %-34s NOT visible after %ss -- checksum will refuse, and that is\n' "still waiting" "$waited"
    printf '    %-34s the right place for it to be decided\n' ""
  fi

  printf '  Accepted. NOTHING IS STAPLED, and that is correct: a bare Mach-O\n'
  printf '  binary has nowhere to hold a ticket. The ticket lives on Apple'"'"'s\n'
  printf '  servers and Gatekeeper checks online. conflab ships exactly this way.\n'
}

# verify_notarised <dir> <file> -- THE ONLY CHECK THAT PROVES THE WHOLE CHAIN,
# and the reason it exists is that every other check passes on a binary that is
# signed but NOT notarised. codesign verifies the signature; spctl -t exec
# answers the wrong question; `stapler` correctly reports no ticket on a bare
# binary either way. **A release that quietly stopped being notarised would sail
# through all of them.**
#
# So this reproduces the only scenario notarisation exists for: a file that
# arrived with the quarantine xattr, as a browser download does. Gatekeeper
# engages ONLY on quarantined files -- which is why an unquarantined check is not
# evidence, and why brew-installed ad-hoc binaries run happily.
#
# IT ASKS ONLY ABOUT STAGED ARTEFACTS, and it used to take a directory so that
# `verify` could ask about the build output while `stage` asked about the shipping
# copies. That parameter is gone with the reason for it: the build output is no
# longer a thing that ever ships, so a verdict about it would be a confident
# answer to a question nobody is asking. **Verify the artefact you are going to
# publish, never its ancestor.**
#
# One implementation, one target, three callers -- which is the arrangement where
# a release-time check cannot drift away from the development-time one everybody
# actually runs.
verify_notarised() {
  local file="$1" t verdict rc=0
  t="$(mktemp -d)"
  /usr/bin/ditto "$STAGE_DIR/$file" "$t/$file"
  # Set quarantine, then ASSERT IT IS SET before reading any verdict: a fixture
  # that never entered the branch proves nothing and looks like a pass.
  xattr -w com.apple.quarantine "0083;0;dc-verify;" "$t/$file"
  if ! xattr -p com.apple.quarantine "$t/$file" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    printf '    %-34s SKIPPED -- could not set the quarantine xattr; verdict withheld\n' "$file"
    rm -rf "$t"
    return 1
  fi
  verdict="$(spctl -a -t open --context context:primary-signature -vv "$t/$file" 2>&1 |
    sed -n 's/^source=//p')"
  if [ "$verdict" = "Notarized Developer ID" ] && (cd "$t" && "./$file" --version >/dev/null 2>&1); then
    printf '    %-34s quarantined, accepted, runs -- source=%s\n' "$file" "$verdict"
  else
    printf '    %-34s FAILED -- source=%s (expected: Notarized Developer ID)\n' "$file" "${verdict:-<none>}"
    rc=1
  fi
  rm -rf "$t"
  return $rc
}

cmd_verify() {
  require_staged
  local triple rc=0
  triple="$(host_triple)"
  for b in $BINARIES; do
    verify_notarised "$b-$triple" || rc=1
  done
  # A QUARANTINED COPY, WHICH IS WHAT WAS MEASURED. This said "from a quarantined
  # download", and nothing here downloads: the file is copied locally and given
  # the quarantine attribute by hand, which is the only local check that means
  # anything. "Download" asserts an origin, and the origin is this machine.
  [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] && { ok; printf '  Signed, notarised, and Gatekeeper-clean from a quarantined copy.\n'; }
  return $rc
}

# cmd_stage -- copy the build output out of shared space, under its shipping name.
#
# THIS IS NOW THE FIRST STEP, NOT THE LAST, and that inversion is the fix for the
# race described at STAGE_DIR above. It used to run after sign and notarise, which
# meant the artefacts were exposed to every peer's `cargo build --release` for the
# whole minutes-long notarisation round trip. Copying FIRST and signing the copies
# means the exposure is this ditto and nothing else.
#
# It deliberately does NOT checksum any more. Checksums belong after signing --
# see cmd_checksum -- and a `stage` that both copied and hashed could only ever
# have hashed unsigned bytes once the order was corrected.
#
# `rm -rf` first, and that is not housekeeping: staging means "start again from
# the build output". Anything already in here is a signature over bytes we are
# about to replace, and keeping it would let a stale artefact survive a rebuild.
cmd_stage() {
  require_binaries
  local triple blockers head sc
  triple="$(host_triple)"

  # RECORDED HERE, REFUSED AT `publish` -- this file's own rule turned on itself.
  # The control goes where the HARM is. A staged tree that cannot name its commit
  # harms nobody: it is how this pipeline gets exercised at all, and refusing here
  # would make every step untestable without a pristine checkout, which is the
  # kind of guard that gets worked around rather than kept. The same bytes in a
  # tap install a build nobody can trace back to anything.
  #
  # THE FIELD IS NAMED FOR WHAT WRITES IT, and it was not. `provenance_blockers`
  # asks `git status`, so this field's subject is THE CHECKOUT -- but it was
  # called `traceable`, which is a word about the ARTEFACTS, and `publish` duly
  # refused on it with a sentence about the artefacts that this file's own
  # `artefact_commit_blockers` contradicts. Renamed rather than re-worded: a
  # sentence can go wrong again, and a field named for its own subject cannot
  # carry the other claim in the first place.
  blockers="$(provenance_blockers "$ROOT")"
  head="$(git -C "$ROOT" rev-parse HEAD 2>/dev/null || printf 'unknown')"
  {
    printf 'commit: %s\n' "$head"
    if [ -z "$blockers" ]; then
      printf 'checkout_clean: yes\n'
    else
      printf 'checkout_clean: no\n'
      printf '%s\n' "$blockers" | sed 's/^/  /'
    fi
  } >"$PROVENANCE_FILE" || die "could not record provenance at $PROVENANCE_FILE"

  step "provenance"
  printf '    %-34s %s\n' "commit" "$head"
  if [ -z "$blockers" ]; then
    printf '    %-34s %s\n' "checkout" "clean"
  else
    printf '    %-34s %s\n' "checkout" "DIRTY -- publish will refuse this"
    printf '%s\n' "$blockers" | sed 's/^/      /'
  fi

  step "staging $BINARIES for $triple"
  rm -rf "$STAGE_DIR"
  mkdir -p "$STAGE_DIR"
  for b in $BINARIES; do
    /usr/bin/ditto "$RELEASE_DIR/$b" "$STAGE_DIR/$b-$triple" ||
      die "could not stage $b"
    printf '    %-34s <- target/release/%s\n' "$b-$triple" "$b"
  done

  # AND NOW ASK THE BYTES THEMSELVES. Everything printed above is a claim about
  # the CHECKOUT, and `stage` used to stop there -- so it reported on binaries it
  # had never asked anything, which is the three-indistinguishable-states defect
  # in this command's own output. `stage` COPIES out of target/release rather than
  # building, and that directory is shared mutable state in a clone several
  # sessions write to, so the bytes it just copied can predate the commit it just
  # recorded by any amount. Measured once at forty-two hours.
  #
  # THIS REPORTS AND DOES NOT REFUSE, deliberately, for the reason given above:
  # the control belongs at publication. A report also cannot be worked around,
  # because it blocks nothing -- and it costs an operator one glance here instead
  # of a signing and notarisation round trip before `publish` tells them.
  #
  # Compared against HEAD, which is exactly why this cannot BE the control: there
  # is no tag at stage time, publishing may legitimately happen after the branch
  # moves on, and a HEAD comparison that REFUSED would refuse every late publish.
  # The shas are printed and the match is DERIVED from them -- a label that states
  # the expected result is a comment, and a comment cannot notice.
  step "what the staged bytes say they were built from"
  for b in $BINARIES; do
    if sc="$(artefact_source_commit "$STAGE_DIR/$b-$triple")"; then
      if [ "$sc" = "$head" ]; then
        printf '    %-34s %s (the commit above)\n' "$b" "$sc"
      else
        printf '    %-34s %s (NOT the commit above)\n' "$b" "$sc"
      fi
    else
      printf '    %-34s no marker -- these bytes name no commit at all\n' "$b"
    fi
  done

  # The support tree. Built HERE, with the binaries, rather than in a later step,
  # because it is an input to the release in exactly the way they are and the
  # ordering hazard that separates the other steps does not apply to it: signing
  # rewrites a binary's bytes, so a hash taken before `sign` is stale, but nothing
  # in this pipeline ever rewrites the tarball. It is built once and hashed once.
  #
  # `--exclude` on .DS_Store, because the Finder writes one into any directory it
  # has been looked at in, and a release artefact whose bytes depend on whether
  # somebody opened a window is not a release artefact.
  step "staging the support tree"

  # REFUSE BEFORE BUILDING, because the archive cannot report what the copy list
  # never asked for. `support_tree_drift` below proves the archive matches
  # SUPPORT_PATHS and stayed green through the whole of 3.0.0 while SUPPORT_PATHS
  # was missing two of the three trees the binary resolves. This is the arm that
  # reads the CONSUMER.
  local uncovered
  uncovered="$(support_paths_coverage)"
  [ -z "$uncovered" ] || die "v3 resolves paths under its install root that $SUPPORT_ASSET would not carry:
$uncovered
       Add them to SUPPORT_PATHS, or to INSTALL_BINARY_PATHS if the formula
       installs them directly. Shipping without them is what put a keg with no
       rule library on the fleet: every critic gate armed 0 of 0 and passed."
  printf '    %-34s %s\n' "resolver coverage" "every install-root path v3 reads is carried"

  tar -czf "$STAGE_DIR/$SUPPORT_ASSET" -C "$ROOT" -s "#^#$SUPPORT_PREFIX/#" \
    --exclude '.DS_Store' $SUPPORT_PATHS ||
    die "could not build $SUPPORT_ASSET from $SUPPORT_PATHS"
  printf '    %-34s <- %s\n' "$SUPPORT_ASSET" "$SUPPORT_PATHS"

  # Assert the archive carries the tree -- DERIVED, not spot-checked. The first
  # version of this listed the six scripts the shipped consumers exec, which is a
  # needle list: it proves the files somebody thought of are present and says
  # nothing about the ones they did not. Comparing the archive against the source
  # tree closes the class instead, and costs less code.
  #
  # Both directions, because they fail differently and both fail quietly. A file
  # in the repo and not the archive ships an install missing a guard; a file in
  # the archive and not the repo means the archive is stale or reaching somewhere
  # it should not.
  local drift
  drift="$(support_tree_drift)"
  [ -z "$drift" ] || die "$SUPPORT_ASSET does not match $SUPPORT_PATHS:
$drift
       '<' is in the source tree and missing from the archive; '>' is in the
       archive and not in the source tree."
  printf '    %-34s matches %s exactly (%s files)\n' "" "$SUPPORT_PATHS" \
    "$(tar -tzf "$STAGE_DIR/$SUPPORT_ASSET" | grep -cv '/$')"

  # EXACTLY ONE TOP-LEVEL ENTRY, AND IT IS THE WRAPPER. This is the property
  # Homebrew's unpack heuristic keys on, and the drift check above cannot see it:
  # drift compares FILE SETS, and today's defect shipped a perfect file set at the
  # wrong depth. Two top-level entries and brew stops stripping; the wrong single
  # entry and it strips the wrong thing. Either way the marker lands at a depth
  # `install::home` does not walk, and NOTHING about the install looks wrong.
  local tops
  tops="$(tar -tzf "$STAGE_DIR/$SUPPORT_ASSET" | awk -F/ 'NF { print $1 }' | sort -u)"
  [ "$tops" = "$SUPPORT_PREFIX" ] ||
    die "$SUPPORT_ASSET must have exactly one top-level entry ('$SUPPORT_PREFIX') so Homebrew's single-directory strip lands the install root -- it has: $(printf '%s' "$tops" | tr '\n' ' ')"
  printf '    %-34s single top-level entry %s/ (Homebrew strips exactly this)\n' "" "$SUPPORT_PREFIX"

  printf '\n'
  ok
  printf '  Staged into %s, which only this command writes.\n' "$STAGE_DIR"
  # SAYS WHAT THIS COMMAND DID, not what the bytes are. "These copies are
  # UNSIGNED" was never measured -- nothing here inspects a signature -- and it is
  # not even reliably true, since an ad-hoc signature IS a signature and
  # `codesign --verify --strict` returns 0 over one.
  printf '  Nothing here has signed these -- they carry whatever the linker applied.\n'
  printf '  NEXT: int macos sign\n'
}

# cmd_checksum -- prove every staged artefact, and only then hash it.
#
# WHY THIS IS A COMMAND AND NOT A NOTE IN A RELEASE DOC. The two operations in a
# release pipeline have OPPOSITE effects on the bytes, and the asymmetry is easy
# to get backwards:
#
#   codesign --force   REWRITES THE BINARY IN PLACE      -> checksum after
#   notarytool submit  uploads a ZIP OF A COPY, no staple -> checksum before or
#                      (a bare Mach-O has nowhere to put   after, both correct
#                      a ticket, so the file is unchanged)
#
# So a sha256 taken before signing is wrong, and it is wrong in the worst
# available way: **it does not fail for us, it fails for every `brew install`**,
# against a formula we published, at the point where we have the least
# visibility. Homebrew's error will say the download is corrupt, which is a
# perfectly misleading description of a checksum computed one step too early.
#
# THE ORDER IS THEREFORE ENFORCED, NOT DOCUMENTED: nothing here computes a
# checksum until `verify_notarised` has passed on the artefact being hashed.
#
# WHAT IT DELETES ON REFUSAL IS THE PROOF, NOT THE WORK. The earlier version wiped
# the whole staging directory, which was harmless when staging was a copy of the
# build output and is actively wrong now that the staged files carry a signature
# and a notarisation. Throwing them away would spend another round trip to Apple
# to rebuild something that was already correct.
#
# But a STALE `SHA256SUMS.txt` cannot be allowed to survive a failure, because
# `formula` treats that file's existence as the proof -- so leaving one behind
# would hand a refused run's output to the next command as if it had passed. The
# artefacts stay; the claim about them goes.
cmd_checksum() {
  require_staged
  local triple rc=0
  triple="$(host_triple)"
  local sums="$STAGE_DIR/SHA256SUMS.txt"

  # EVERY STAGED ARTEFACT IS CLASSIFIED, AND AN UNCLASSIFIED ONE IS A REFUSAL.
  #
  # This command used to have one set -- "the staged files" -- and treat it as
  # both "what must be proven notarised" and "what gets hashed", which was correct
  # only while every artefact was a Mach-O. The support tarball breaks that
  # identity: it must be hashed and it cannot be notarised.
  #
  # The dangerous way to write that is a special case for the tarball, because a
  # special case is a needle list and a needle list forbids only what its author
  # thought of. **The next artefact after this one gets a decision or it gets
  # refused** -- it cannot slip in as unproven bytes under a published hash, and
  # it cannot be silently omitted from SHA256SUMS.txt the way the old
  # `*-$triple` glob would have omitted it.
  local unclassified
  unclassified="$(unclassified_artefacts "$triple")"
  [ -z "$unclassified" ] || {
    rm -f "$sums"
    die "unclassified staged artefact(s):$unclassified
       Every artefact is either a Mach-O that MUST be proven signed and notarised
       (add it to BINARIES) or data that cannot be (add it beside SUPPORT_ASSET).
       Refusing to guess: guessing wrong publishes either an unproven binary or a
       hash for a file nobody checked."
  }

  step "proving every Mach-O artefact before anything is hashed"
  for b in $BINARIES; do
    verify_notarised "$b-$triple" || rc=1
  done
  if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then
    rm -f "$sums"
    die "refusing to checksum -- a staged artefact is not signed AND notarised. Run: int macos sign && int macos notarize (the staged binaries are left alone; only the checksum claim is withdrawn)"
  fi
  printf '    %-34s not a Mach-O -- nothing to sign, nothing to notarise\n' "$SUPPORT_ASSET"

  # Hashed from the CLASSIFIED set, never from a glob. The old form globbed
  # `*-$triple`, which silently omits any artefact not spelled that way -- and an
  # asset missing from SHA256SUMS.txt is one `formula` cannot name and `publish`
  # cannot round-trip, so it would ship unverified or not at all.
  step "checksums"
  (cd "$STAGE_DIR" && staged_artefacts | tr '\n' '\0' | xargs -0 shasum -a 256 >SHA256SUMS.txt) ||
    die "could not write SHA256SUMS.txt"

  printf '\n'
  ok
  printf '  staged: %s\n\n' "$STAGE_DIR"
  sed 's/^/    /' "$sums"
  # PER ARTEFACT, BECAUSE THE SET IS NOT UNIFORM. This said "every line above is
  # a hash of a binary this command has just proven signed and notarised" -- and
  # one line is the support tarball, which is not a Mach-O, was never signed and
  # never notarised, and is stated as the exception twelve lines up before the
  # summary asserted the rule over it. A summary that generalises past its own
  # exception is an unmeasured assertion in the place people read first.
  printf '\n  Each BINARY above was proven signed and notarised from a quarantined\n'
  printf '  copy before it was hashed. %s is not a Mach-O: it carries no\n' "$SUPPORT_ASSET"
  printf '  signature and no ticket, and nothing here claims it does -- its hash\n'
  printf '  says the bytes are intact and says nothing about their origin.\n'
  printf '  NOT REGENERATED ON RE-SIGN -- re-run `checksum` after any `sign`,\n'
  printf '  because signing rewrites the bytes these hashes describe.\n'
}

# cmd_prepare -- the whole sequence, as one command, because it has to be one.
#
# THE OPERATOR'S MEMORY IS NOT A CONTROL. Every individual subcommand here is
# correct in isolation and the failure this exists to prevent is not a bug in any
# of them -- it is the GAP BETWEEN them. Four commands run by hand, with a
# multi-minute wait in the middle, is four chances to stop halfway and come back
# to a tree a peer has rebuilt underneath you. That is not hypothetical; it is
# what happened, and the note I wrote afterwards said "must run as one
# uninterrupted sequence", which is a reminder, and reminders are not load-bearing.
#
# The subcommands stay individually callable, for diagnosis and for re-running one
# step. This is the path anyone cutting a release actually takes.
cmd_prepare() {
  cmd_stage
  printf '\n'
  cmd_sign
  printf '\n'
  cmd_notarize
  printf '\n'
  cmd_checksum
  printf '\n'
  # NAMES THE STEPS THAT RAN, not a property of every artefact they touched --
  # `sign` and `notarize` never touch the support tarball, so the participle list
  # was asserting over an artefact two of its four words do not apply to.
  printf 'prepare: stage, sign, notarize and checksum ran in one pass; each step\n'
  printf '  reported above what it did and did not cover.\n'
  printf '  NEXT: int macos formula\n'
}

# cmd_formula -- emit the Homebrew formula from the staged artefacts.
#
# GENERATED, NEVER HAND-EDITED, and that is the whole point. A tap formula
# carries a version and one sha256 per artefact, and every one of them is a
# restatement of a fact that already exists somewhere else. Conflab hand-maintains
# exactly these lines and has a `release sync` command partly to heal the drift
# that follows. **A number copied by hand is a number that will eventually be
# copied wrong**, and this particular wrong number does not fail for us -- it
# fails for every `brew install`, against a formula we already published.
#
# IT INHERITS `stage`'s REFUSAL STRUCTURALLY RATHER THAN RE-CHECKING IT. The only
# input is `SHA256SUMS.txt`, which `stage` writes ONLY for artefacts it has proven
# signed and notarised from a quarantined copy. So there is no path from an
# unproven binary to a formula: not because this command checks again, but because
# the file it needs does not exist. One check, one place, no second opinion to
# drift out of step.
#
# THE DEV-VERSION REFUSAL IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE. Emitting a formula that reads
# `version "3.0.0-dev"` is harmless -- it is text on a terminal, and it is
# self-evidently not a release. The harm is in PUBLISHING one, so the refusal
# belongs in the publish step. **Put the control where the harm is, not where it
# is convenient to write.**
#
# VALIDATED WITH `brew style`, AND THE CALIBRATION IS THE POINT. Linted as a bare
# file the generated formula reports five offences; linted AT A TAP PATH it
# reports one. Four of the five were artefacts of judging a formula by the
# generic Ruby config -- Sorbet sigils, a frozen-string-literal comment,
# top-level class documentation -- none of which Homebrew applies inside a tap.
# **Calibrated against `geodica/homebrew-conflab`'s live shipped formula**, which
# reports the same one offence and none of the four, then confirmed by linting a
# copy of ours at a real tap path. `Style/MutableConstant` is now fixed here, so
# this generates a formula that lints clean where the reference does not.
#
# **A linter run in the wrong context reports real-looking findings about a
# correct artefact**, and the only way to tell was to run it against something
# already known good. Same shape as `spctl -a -t exec` calling a correctly signed
# binary rejected.
#
# THE CONSTANT ITSELF STAYS, deliberately. `#{version}` may well work in the URL
# and would remove the second copy, but the constant is what the shipped conflab
# formula does, this path has no end-to-end test, and refactoring an unexercised
# artefact for elegance is how the first real run finds something new. The two
# copies come from one shell variable, so they cannot drift.
#
# EXPIRY, stated rather than left to be noticed: this lives under `macos` because
# hv ruled 2026-08-15 that v3.0.0 ships macOS arm64 ONLY, which makes the formula
# a single-platform document. If Linux artefacts are ever added the formula grows
# a second platform block and stops being a macOS concern -- move it then.
# require_sums -- the proof file, or the reason there isn't one.
#
# ONE MESSAGE FOR ONE MISSING FILE. `formula` and `publish` both need it and both
# need to say the same thing about why it is absent, because the answer is never
# "create it" -- it is "prove the artefacts and it will be written for you".
require_sums() {
  [ -f "$STAGE_DIR/SHA256SUMS.txt" ] ||
    die "no proven artefacts to describe -- run 'int macos prepare' first (SHA256SUMS.txt is written only by 'checksum', and only for artefacts it has proven signed AND notarised)"
}

# staged_version -- the version the SHIPPING binary reports.
#
# From the BINARY, never from Cargo.toml or a git tag. It is the only source that
# cannot disagree with what a user will actually run: a version read from a
# manifest describes what was meant to be built, and the gap between those two is
# exactly what a release must not ship. It also makes the formula's `test do`
# block pass by construction rather than by coincidence.
staged_version() {
  local v
  # THE VERSION TOKEN ONLY. `--version` prints `intent <version> (<commit>)`, and
  # the provenance suffix is NOT part of the version. Taking everything after
  # `intent ` put `3.0.0-dev (4c5babdf...)` into `version`, into RELEASE_VERSION,
  # and therefore into every download URL -- a URL carrying a space and two
  # parentheses, inside a formula that was otherwise perfectly well-formed and
  # linted clean. Nothing about the output looked wrong.
  #
  # FOUND ON THE FIRST END-TO-END RUN, which is precisely what the cmd_formula
  # header predicted would happen to this path: "this path has no end-to-end
  # test, and refactoring an unexercised artefact for elegance is how the first
  # real run finds something new". It found something new.
  v="$("$STAGE_DIR/intent-$(host_triple)" --version 2>/dev/null | sed -n 's/^intent \([^ ]*\).*/\1/p')"
  [ -n "$v" ] || die "could not read a version from the staged intent binary"
  # A VERSION THAT CANNOT SIT IN A URL IS A BROKEN FORMULA, and it does not break
  # for us -- it breaks for every `brew install`, against a formula already
  # published. Refuse where the harm is, the same rule that keeps the dev-version
  # refusal in `publish`.
  case "$v" in
    *[!A-Za-z0-9.+_-]*)
      die "the staged binary reports a version that is not URL-safe: '$v' -- the formula's download URLs are built from it"
      ;;
  esac
  printf '%s' "$v"
}

cmd_formula() {
  local stage_dir="$STAGE_DIR" triple version sums revision=0 revline=""

  # `--revision N` -- HOMEBREW'S ANSWER TO "THE FORMULA WAS WRONG AND THE
  # VERSION IS NOT". A formula fixed at an unchanged version needs a revision
  # bump or an existing install never picks the fix up; brew treats
  # (version, revision) as the identity. THE KNOB LIVES HERE RATHER THAN IN THE
  # EMITTED FILE because the tap file is GENERATED -- hand-editing a revision in
  # is the same class of defect as hand-editing a sha256, and it survives
  # exactly until the next `int macos formula` regenerates over it.
  while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
    case "$1" in
      --revision)
        shift
        revision="${1:-}"
        case "$revision" in
          ''|*[!0-9]*) die "--revision takes a non-negative integer, got '${revision:-<empty>}'" ;;
        esac
        ;;
      *) die "unknown formula option: $1 (only --revision N)" ;;
    esac
    shift
  done

  triple="$(host_triple)"
  sums="$stage_dir/SHA256SUMS.txt"
  require_sums
  version="$(staged_version)"

  # Omitted entirely at 0, which is what Homebrew expects: `revision 0` is not
  # idiomatic and `brew style` reads it as noise. The trailing newline keeps the
  # blank line before RELEASE_VERSION in both branches.
  if [ "$revision" -gt 0 ]; then
    revline="  revision $revision
"
  fi

  local sha_cli sha_daemon sha_support
  sha_cli="$(awk -v f="intent-$triple" '$2 == f { print $1 }' "$sums")"
  sha_daemon="$(awk -v f="intentd-$triple" '$2 == f { print $1 }' "$sums")"
  sha_support="$(awk -v f="$SUPPORT_ASSET" '$2 == f { print $1 }' "$sums")"
  [ -n "$sha_cli" ] && [ -n "$sha_daemon" ] && [ -n "$sha_support" ] ||
    die "$sums does not carry a hash for all three artefacts -- re-run 'int macos prepare'"

  cat <<FORMULA
class Intent < Formula
  desc "Steel thread process for helping LLMs help you work with your code"
  homepage "https://github.com/matthewsinclair/intent"
  version "$version"
  license "MIT"
${revline}
  RELEASE_VERSION = "$version".freeze

  # macOS arm64 only, by ruling (hv, 2026-08-15) rather than by omission. Both
  # binaries are Developer ID signed and notarised; nothing is stapled, because a
  # bare Mach-O has nowhere to hold a ticket and Gatekeeper checks online.
  on_macos do
    on_arm do
      url "https://github.com/matthewsinclair/intent/releases/download/v#{RELEASE_VERSION}/intent-$triple"
      sha256 "$sha_cli"

      resource "intentd" do
        url "https://github.com/matthewsinclair/intent/releases/download/v#{RELEASE_VERSION}/intentd-$triple"
        sha256 "$sha_daemon"
      end

      # The hooks and guards the binaries exec. Not signed and not notarised --
      # there is no Mach-O in it. See SUPPORT_ASSET in bin/.devbin/cmd/macos.
      resource "support" do
        url "https://github.com/matthewsinclair/intent/releases/download/v#{RELEASE_VERSION}/$SUPPORT_ASSET"
        sha256 "$sha_support"
      end
    end
  end

  # EVERYTHING LANDS IN libexec AND bin GETS SYMLINKS, which is not the obvious
  # layout and is not a style preference.
  #
  # \`intent\` locates its own install by walking up from its symlink-resolved
  # location to the directory containing \`lib/templates/\`, so the support tree
  # has to sit beside the binary inside the keg -- a plain \`bin.install\` leaves
  # the binary unable to find the hooks it execs, and both whiteboard guards and
  # every session hook stop working with nothing reporting a failure.
  #
  # That rules IN two layouts and Homebrew rules one of them OUT. Installing to
  # \`prefix/lib/templates\` resolves correctly, but \`lib\` is a LINKED directory:
  # brew symlinks keg \`lib\` subdirectories into the shared prefix, so this would
  # publish a directory called \`templates\` into \`#{HOMEBREW_PREFIX}/lib\`
  # alongside 858 other formulae's entries. \`templates\` is about as generic as a
  # name gets, and the collision would surface as a \`brew link\` failure in
  # somebody else's install. \`libexec\` is not linked, so the tree stays private
  # to this keg.
  def install
    (libexec/"bin").install Dir["intent-*"].first => "intent"
    resource("intentd").stage do
      (libexec/"bin").install Dir["intentd-*"].first => "intentd"
    end
    # The archive is rooted at the install root, so this stays correct when the
    # shipped set grows -- it installs whatever the tarball carries rather than
    # naming \`templates\` and having to change when something joins it.
    resource("support").stage do
      libexec.install Dir["*"]
    end

    # THE EXECUTABLE BIT, AND IT IS NOT BELT-AND-BRACES -- WITHOUT IT THE KEG
    # INSTALLS AND NOTHING IN IT RUNS. GitHub serves release assets as plain
    # files with no exec bit, and Homebrew's \`.install\` PRESERVES the source
    # mode rather than setting one. So 644 goes in, 644 comes out, \`brew
    # install\` prints its beer emoji, and every \`intent\` call returns
    # \`permission denied\`. Measured on the v3.0.0 tap, 2026-08-26, on the first
    # real install from the network.
    #
    # A CACHE-PRESEEDED INSTALL CANNOT CATCH THIS, which is why it survived
    # every proof taken before that one: a preseed fills the cache from LOCAL
    # files that are already +x, so the mode survives and the install works.
    # Only the network hop loses it. "Proves everything but the network hop"
    # was an honest scoping, and this is what was behind it.
    chmod 0755, libexec/"bin/intent"
    chmod 0755, libexec/"bin/intentd"

    bin.install_symlink libexec/"bin/intent"
    bin.install_symlink libexec/"bin/intentd"
  end

  # NO \`service do\` BLOCK YET, and its absence is deliberate. intentd currently
  # reports "not yet implemented": there is no start verb and no log path, so any
  # block here would be a guess at an interface that does not exist. It arrives
  # with the daemon lifecycle.

  test do
    assert_match version.to_s, shell_output("#{bin}/intent --version")
  end
end
FORMULA

  printf '\n' >&2
  printf 'generated from %s\n' "$sums" >&2
  printf 'The URLs above assume a release tagged v%s exists on matthewsinclair/intent\n' "$version" >&2
  printf 'carrying these exact artefacts. Publish the release BEFORE the formula.\n' >&2
}

# cmd_publish -- put the proven artefacts where a user can reach them, and only
# then make the claim that points at them.
#
# THE ORDER IS THE DESIGN. Upload, then DOWNLOAD WHAT WAS UPLOADED AND HASH IT,
# then write the formula. AC-11.4 is not "we computed a checksum correctly", it
# is "the published checksum matches the bytes the user downloads" -- and the
# only way to assert that is to be the user for one round trip. Hashing the local
# file and publishing that number asserts the same thing about a file nobody will
# ever fetch. The extra cost is one download; the class of defect it removes is
# the one that never fails for us and fails for every `brew install`.
#
# AND IF THE DOWNLOAD DISAGREES, THE RELEASE STAYS AND THE FORMULA DOES NOT SHIP.
# That asymmetry is deliberate. A release with no formula pointing at it is
# inert -- nobody installs it by accident. A formula pointing at bytes whose hash
# we could not confirm is a confident false statement, and it is the same
# reasoning that keeps the tap deliberately empty rather than carrying a formula
# for a release that does not exist: **a wrong artefact is not a neutral
# placeholder.**
#
# THE DEV-VERSION REFUSAL LIVES HERE, WHERE THE HARM IS. `formula` will happily
# print `version "3.0.0-dev"` to a terminal, and should -- that is text, and it is
# self-evidently not a release. The same bytes in a tap install a build nobody
# meant to ship. **A control belongs at the point of harm, not at the first point
# it could have been written.**
#
# IT DOES NOT CREATE THE TAG, and that is a boundary rather than an omission. A
# tag is the release cut's act; this command publishes artefacts for a cut that
# has already happened. A publisher that silently tags is doing half of somebody
# else's job, and the half it does is the irreversible one.
cmd_publish() {
  local dry=0
  case "${1:-}" in
    --dry-run) dry=1 ;;
    "") ;;
    *) die "unknown publish option: $1 (only --dry-run)" ;;
  esac

  require_tool gh "Install it with: brew install gh"
  require_tool curl
  require_staged
  require_sums

  local triple version tag
  triple="$(host_triple)"
  version="$(staged_version)"
  tag="v$version"

  step "the version the STAGED BINARY reports: $version"

  # One predicate for "is this a release version", borrowed rather than
  # restated: assert_semver is the estate's answer and it dies on anything that
  # is not X.Y.Z. The subshell contains its exit so this can own the message,
  # because the reason publishing is refused is not the reason a version is
  # malformed.
  if ! (assert_semver "$version") 2>/dev/null; then
    die "refusing to publish '$version' -- this is a development build, not a release.
       A formula in the tap is not a document, it is an INSTALLER: whatever it
       names is what 'brew install intent' puts on a stranger's machine. Printing
       one is harmless and 'int macos formula' will still do it.
       Set a real version in native/rust/Cargo.toml, rebuild, and re-run
       'int macos prepare' so the staged binary reports it."
  fi

  # Refuse to republish. Overwriting the assets of a release a formula already
  # points at changes the bytes under a hash that has been published as true --
  # the precise failure this command exists to prevent, committed by the command
  # itself.
  #
  # **THE TEST IS ASSETS, NOT EXISTENCE, AND THAT DISTINCTION IS THE WHOLE v3
  # RELEASE.** This check used to refuse any release with this tag, which is
  # broader than the reason above and made a v3 cut impossible: `int build
  # release` creates the tag AND the GitHub release in one step (`gh release
  # create`, build.d/release:531), while the next line here requires the tag to
  # already exist because the cut owns tagging. So publish demanded a state --
  # tag present, release absent -- that the only thing which creates the tag
  # cannot leave behind. **Each command was individually correct and the
  # composition was impossible**, which is the failure this estate keeps finding
  # in the gaps between correct steps rather than inside them.
  #
  # A release carrying ZERO assets has published no bytes and no hash describes
  # anything, so attaching to it violates nothing the comment above protects.
  # One that carries assets is refused exactly as before.
  #
  # FAILS CLOSED. If the asset count cannot be read, the question "have bytes
  # already been published under this tag" is unanswered, and a publisher that
  # proceeds while it is unanswered is the failure wearing a different hat.
  local have_release=0
  if gh release view "$tag" --repo "$REPO" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    local n
    # Assignment on its own line: `local n="$(...)"` returns local's status, not
    # the command's, so a failing gh would read as success.
    n="$(gh release view "$tag" --repo "$REPO" --json assets -q '.assets | length' 2>/dev/null)" || n=""
    case "$n" in
      0)
        have_release=1
        ;;
      '' | *[!0-9]*)
        die "could not read the asset count for $REPO $tag. Refusing to publish while it is unknown whether bytes have already been published under this tag -- check 'gh auth status'."
        ;;
      *)
        die "$REPO already has a release $tag carrying $n asset(s). Refusing to overwrite published bytes: a hash that has already been published as true must keep describing the same file. Cut a new version."
        ;;
    esac
  fi

  git -C "$ROOT" rev-parse -q --verify "refs/tags/$tag" >/dev/null 2>&1 ||
    die "no tag $tag in this repository. The release cut owns tagging; this command only publishes artefacts for a cut that has already happened."

  # THE BYTES MUST COME FROM THE TAG THEY ARE PUBLISHED UNDER, and until now
  # nothing here asked. Every other check in this command asks whether these
  # bytes agree with EACH OTHER -- the round trip re-downloads what it uploaded
  # and hashes THAT, `formula` reads the sums file, `checksum` refuses an
  # unclassified artefact. All of that is internal consistency, and a set of
  # bytes built from a peer's uncommitted work is perfectly self-consistent.
  #
  # Two failures it closes, both reachable today. `stage` COPIES out of
  # native/rust/target/release rather than building, and that directory is shared
  # mutable state in a clone several sessions write to. And the support tarball is
  # archived from the WORKING TREE, so uncommitted shell ships verbatim.
  #
  # Compared against the TAG's commit rather than HEAD: publishing is allowed to
  # happen after the branch has moved on, and what matters is that the artefacts
  # match the thing being published, not the thing being worked on.
  local prov_commit prov_clean tag_commit
  [ -f "$PROVENANCE_FILE" ] ||
    die "no provenance record at $PROVENANCE_FILE -- run 'int macos stage' (or 'prepare'), which writes it. Refusing to publish bytes whose source commit was never recorded."
  prov_commit="$(sed -n 's/^commit: //p' "$PROVENANCE_FILE" | head -1)"
  prov_clean="$(sed -n 's/^checkout_clean: //p' "$PROVENANCE_FILE" | head -1)"
  # EVERY REFUSAL NAMES THE SUBJECT IT MEASURED, and this one did not. It read
  # `git status` and said "the staged artefacts cannot name their source commit"
  # -- a sentence about the BYTES. Driven, three cases, real repositories: with
  # ONE unrelated file dirty and both binaries built at HEAD, this refused with a
  # claim that `artefact_commit_blockers` twenty-eight lines below contradicts,
  # and its remedy sent the operator to rebuild binaries that were never the
  # problem. Not an edge case either -- the wide arm trips on dirt anywhere, so in
  # a clone several sessions write to this is the ORDINARY message.
  #
  # An unreachable correct answer is the other half: this gate dies before the
  # artefact gate runs, so in that case the check that could have answered was
  # never reached. Left in this order deliberately -- the support tarball is
  # archived from the working tree, so tree dirt is its own harm and not merely a
  # proxy for stale bytes. What changed is that the sentence now says so.
  [ "$prov_clean" = "yes" ] ||
    die "the working tree was not clean when these artefacts were staged. $PROVENANCE_FILE records:
$(sed 's/^/       /' "$PROVENANCE_FILE")
       Commit the work and stage again. Whether the BINARIES came from this tag
       is a separate question with a separate check, below, read off the bytes.
       This is refused at publish rather than at stage because a staged tree
       nobody ships is harmless; these bytes would go into a tap."
  tag_commit="$(git -C "$ROOT" rev-list -n 1 "$tag" 2>/dev/null)"
  [ -n "$tag_commit" ] ||
    die "could not resolve $tag to a commit, having just verified the tag exists -- refusing while the repository state is this confusing."
  # SAYS WHAT IT MEASURED, WHICH IS WHERE THE STAGING RAN. `$prov_commit` is
  # `git rev-parse HEAD` at stage time, so it is a fact about the CHECKOUT; this
  # message used to say the artefacts "were built at" it, which nothing here
  # established -- the same defect as the gate above, one step down, found by
  # sweeping every surface rather than by fixing the one that was reported.
  [ "$prov_commit" = "$tag_commit" ] ||
    die "these artefacts were staged from a checkout at $prov_commit, but $tag is $tag_commit.
       Publishing them would attach bytes to a tag they did not come from, and every
       hash in the pipeline would still agree with every other hash. Re-stage at $tag.
       What the BYTES were built from is a separate question and is read off the
       bytes themselves, below -- a checkout can sit at the tag over any binaries."

  # THE PIPELINE'S ONLY CHECK WHOSE SUBJECT IS THE ARTEFACT, and that is the
  # claim to hold rather than "a provenance field was added" (cc's sharpening,
  # taken because it changes what the check is FOR). Everything above this line
  # -- `traceable`, `prov_commit` against `tag_commit`, the round trip that
  # re-downloads and re-hashes -- takes its input from the CHECKOUT or from the
  # pipeline's own earlier output. None of them can disagree with the others, so
  # their unanimity carries no information about the bytes.
  #
  # AND NO INDIVIDUAL CHECK ABOVE IS WRONG, which is why nobody noticed and why
  # the remedy could never have been to check harder. Each is correct and each is
  # necessary; the pipeline simply had one SUBJECT while appearing to have
  # several. The number of controls says nothing -- the number of distinct
  # subjects does.
  local artefact_blockers
  artefact_blockers="$(artefact_commit_blockers "$STAGE_DIR" "$(host_triple)" "$tag_commit")"
  [ -z "$artefact_blockers" ] ||
    die "the staged BYTES cannot be shown to have come from $tag ($tag_commit):
$(printf '%s\n' "$artefact_blockers" | sed 's/^/       /')
       This is read from each artefact's own embedded source commit, not from any
       record written beside it, so it is the one check here a self-consistent
       build from the wrong tree cannot satisfy. Rebuild at $tag and re-stage."

  # THE POSITIVE CONTROL, PRINTED ON EVERY PASS. A blocker function that fired on
  # nothing because it examined nothing reads exactly like one that passed, and
  # this file already carries that lesson about `provenance_blockers`. Naming
  # each binary and the commit it claims is what says the check ran.
  local pb pbc
  for pb in $BINARIES; do
    pbc="$(artefact_source_commit "$STAGE_DIR/$pb-$(host_triple)")" || pbc="<none>"
    printf '    %-34s names %s\n' "$pb" "$pbc"
  done

  # THE RELEASE BODY MAKES A PERMANENT PUBLIC CLAIM, SO IT IS MEASURED HERE AND
  # NOT INHERITED. `gh release create` below writes "Both binaries are Developer
  # ID signed and notarised" onto the releases page, where it outlives every
  # terminal this pipeline ever printed to -- and until now `publish` established
  # neither half. It leaned on `checksum` having refused, which is a claim about
  # an earlier step surviving in a record beside the artefacts. That is the exact
  # shape AC-11.5 forbids, and it sat on the most public surface in the pipeline.
  #
  # `verify_notarised` and nothing else, so this remains ONE implementation with
  # three consumers rather than a second copy that drifts: it is the only check
  # that proves the whole chain, on a quarantined copy, because `codesign
  # --verify --strict` returns 0 over the linker's ad-hoc signature.
  step "proving the claim the release body is about to make"
  local vrc=0 vb
  for vb in $BINARIES; do
    verify_notarised "$vb-$(host_triple)" || vrc=1
  done
  [ "$vrc" -eq 0 ] ||
    die "refusing to publish: the release notes assert both binaries are Developer ID
       signed and notarised, and that could not be established on these bytes just
       now. Nothing has been uploaded. Re-run 'int macos prepare' -- and note that
       signing REWRITES a binary, so a re-sign after 'checksum' leaves hashes that
       describe bytes no longer on disk."

  gh repo view "$TAP" >/dev/null 2>&1 ||
    die "cannot reach the tap $TAP -- check 'gh auth status'"

  # UPLOAD THE HASHED SET, NEVER A RESTATED ONE. Both this list and the
  # round-trip below iterate what `checksum` actually classified and hashed, so
  # "what gets published" and "what has a published hash" cannot drift apart. The
  # earlier form listed $BINARIES here and would have shipped two of the three
  # artefacts, silently, leaving the formula pointing at a resource that 404s.
  local a assets=""
  for a in $(staged_artefacts); do assets="$assets $STAGE_DIR/$a"; done

  step "plan"
  if [ "$have_release" -eq 1 ]; then
    printf '    release   %s %s (EXISTS, no assets -- attaching to the cut'"'"'s release)\n' "$REPO" "$tag"
  else
    printf '    release   %s %s (will be created)\n' "$REPO" "$tag"
  fi
  for a in $(staged_artefacts); do printf '    artefact  %s\n' "$a"; done
  printf '    tap       %s (Formula/intent.rb)\n' "$TAP"

  if [ "$dry" -eq 1 ]; then
    printf '\n'
    printf 'dry run: nothing was published. Every check above passed.\n'
    printf '  The steps NOT run: create or attach to the release, upload, re-download and hash,\n'
    printf '  and push the formula. Re-run without --dry-run to do them.\n'
    return 0
  fi

  if [ "$have_release" -eq 1 ]; then
    # The cut already made the release; this only adds the artefacts to it.
    step "uploading artefacts to the existing $tag on $REPO"
    # shellcheck disable=SC2086
    gh release upload "$tag" $assets --repo "$REPO" ||
      die "could not upload the artefacts to $tag"
  else
    step "creating $tag on $REPO"
    # shellcheck disable=SC2086
    gh release create "$tag" $assets --repo "$REPO" --title "Intent $tag" --notes "Intent $version -- macOS arm64. Both binaries are Developer ID signed and notarised." ||
      die "could not create the release"
  fi
  ok

  # BE THE USER. Same public URL a formula sends brew to.
  step "re-downloading the published artefacts and hashing what came back"
  local tmp name rc=0
  tmp="$(mktemp -d)"
  for name in $(staged_artefacts); do
    local want got
    want="$(awk -v f="$name" '$2 == f { print $1 }' "$STAGE_DIR/SHA256SUMS.txt")"
    # An artefact with no line in SHA256SUMS.txt has just been uploaded with no
    # published hash describing it. Comparing "" against a real hash would report
    # a MISMATCH, which is the right outcome reached for the wrong reason and
    # names the wrong fix; say what is actually wrong.
    [ -n "$want" ] ||
      die "$name was uploaded but SHA256SUMS.txt carries no hash for it -- re-run 'int macos prepare' and publish a fresh tag."
    curl -fsSL -o "$tmp/$name" "https://github.com/$REPO/releases/download/$tag/$name" ||
      die "could not download the artefact we just published: $name"
    got="$(shasum -a 256 "$tmp/$name" | awk '{ print $1 }')"
    if [ "$want" = "$got" ]; then
      printf '    %-34s %s\n' "$name" "matches"
    else
      printf '    %-34s MISMATCH\n      published: %s\n      expected:  %s\n' "$name" "$got" "$want"
      rc=1
    fi
  done
  rm -rf "$tmp"

  if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then
    die "the bytes GitHub serves do not hash to what we published as their checksum.
       The release $tag is left in place and NO FORMULA HAS BEEN PUSHED, so nothing
       installs these bytes. Delete the release, re-run 'int macos prepare', and
       publish again."
  fi

  step "pushing the formula to $TAP"
  local formula work
  formula="$(cmd_formula 2>/dev/null)" || die "could not generate the formula"
  work="$(mktemp -d)"
  gh repo clone "$TAP" "$work/tap" -- --quiet >/dev/null 2>&1 || die "could not clone $TAP"
  mkdir -p "$work/tap/Formula"
  printf '%s\n' "$formula" >"$work/tap/Formula/intent.rb"
  git -C "$work/tap" add Formula/intent.rb
  git -C "$work/tap" commit -q -m "intent $version

Generated by 'int macos publish' from artefacts proven signed and notarised,
and re-downloaded from the published release to confirm the hashes describe the
bytes a user actually receives.

(C) hello@matthewsinclair.com" || die "nothing to commit -- the formula is unchanged"
  git -C "$work/tap" push -q origin HEAD || die "could not push the formula to $TAP"
  rm -rf "$work"
  ok

  printf '\n'
  printf 'published: %s %s, artefacts verified from the published URL, formula live in the tap.\n' "$REPO" "$tag"
  printf '  STILL OWED, and this command cannot supply it: an install run on a machine\n'
  printf '  that has never seen this repository. Everything above proves the artefacts\n'
  printf '  and the formula agree; only that proves the tap works for a stranger.\n'
  printf '    brew install %s/intent\n' "$BREW_TAP"
}

# smoke -- the only check whose subject is the INSTALLED KEG.
#
# WHY IT IS NOT `brew test`. The formula carries a `test do` asserting exactly
# the thing that shipped broken, and `brew install` never runs it. `brew test`
# on an UNLINKED keg returns `not linked` and cannot gate a flip (vc's ruling),
# so this drives `$KEG/bin/intent` directly.
#
# WHY IT IS NOT DESTRUCTIVE BY DEFAULT, WHICH IS A CHANGE FROM ITS REGISTRATION.
# The row says "installs cold from the tap"; on this machine that would
# `brew uninstall` the binary FIVE CONCURRENT SESSIONS ARE USING, mid-run, to
# test it. A check that breaks the estate to prove the estate works is not a
# check. So the cold install is `--reinstall`, opt-in and announced, and the
# default asserts against whatever keg is installed. What that costs is stated
# rather than hidden: without `--reinstall` this cannot tell a cold network
# install from a cache-preseeded one, and the preseed is precisely what hid the
# 644 defect -- so the default proves the keg on disk is good and says nothing
# about how it got there.
# COUNT THE FAILURES, NEVER THE LINES. The first version reported
# `grep -c '^'` over the accumulated text and said "fails 6 check(s)" while
# listing 3 -- one message carried the multi-line output of the command it was
# reporting on. A miscount reads exactly as authoritative as a correct one.
smoke_fail() {
  SMOKE_FAIL_COUNT=$((SMOKE_FAIL_COUNT + 1))
  SMOKE_FAILURES="$SMOKE_FAILURES       - $1
"
  printf '    %-46s FAIL\n' "$2"
}

smoke_ok() { printf '    %-46s ok\n' "$1"; }

# smoke_views_fixture <binary> <dir> -- build ARM 7's subject, or leave no sentinel.
#
# IT NEVER FAILS THE RUN AND NEVER REPORTS SUCCESS IT DID NOT HAVE. The file is
# `set -euo pipefail`, so a fixture step that exits non-zero would kill the whole
# command between arms and read exactly like a pass. So the body runs in a
# subshell swallowed by `|| true`, and the ONLY evidence of success is the
# sentinel written by its last line. The caller tests the sentinel, never an
# exit code.
#
# THE GIT IDENTITY IS PASSED PER-COMMAND rather than assumed: on a machine with
# no global user.email the commit would fail and take the fixture with it, and
# the arm would report a build failure that is really a configuration one.
#
# BOTH IGNORES ARE LOAD-BEARING. `intent/.cache/` keeps the store and its WAL out
# of the assertion -- they are not views and their churn would swamp it. The
# sentinel is ignored because it is written INTO the fixture and would otherwise
# be untracked dirt in the very tree the next arm asserts is clean.
smoke_views_fixture() {
  local bin="$1" dir="$2"
  (
    set -e
    cd "$dir"
    "$bin" init smokefix
    "$bin" st new "A thread the read verb must name"
    printf 'intent/.cache/\n.fixture-ok\n' > .gitignore
    git init -q .
    git add -A
    git -c user.name=smoke -c user.email=smoke@fixture.invalid commit -qm base
    # The divergence: bytes in a TRACKED view that the store did not render.
    # A convergent fixture cannot exhibit a re-render, so it would pass under a
    # binary that rewrites every view on every read.
    printf '\n<!-- foreign line the store never rendered -->\n' >> intent/st/steel_threads.md
    git add intent/st/steel_threads.md
    git -c user.name=smoke -c user.email=smoke@fixture.invalid commit -qm diverge
    : > .fixture-ok
  ) >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
}

cmd_smoke() {
  local reinstall=0 keg_override=""
  while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
    case "$1" in
      --reinstall) reinstall=1 ;;
      # `--keg <dir>` EXISTS SO THE GREEN PATH CAN BE DRIVEN WITHOUT A RELEASE,
      # following this file's own precedent for `unclassified_artefacts`: a
      # check whose first exercise is a release is one nobody has tested. It is
      # also the only way to prove the green path on a shared machine -- the
      # alternative is copying trees into the LIVE keg, which would fix the
      # fleet's critic gates by hand and leave the keg silently disagreeing with
      # what its formula installs. That is the drift class, not a repair.
      --keg) shift; keg_override="${1:-}"
        [ -n "$keg_override" ] || die "--keg needs a directory" ;;
      *) die "unknown smoke option: $1 (only --reinstall, --keg <dir>)" ;;
    esac
    shift
  done

  SMOKE_FAILURES=""
  SMOKE_FAIL_COUNT=0
  local keg libexec bin_intent bin_daemon out rc tmp mode f h

  if [ "$reinstall" -eq 1 ]; then
    step "reinstalling cold from the tap -- THIS UNLINKS THE BINARY OTHER SESSIONS ARE USING"
    brew uninstall --force intent >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
    brew untap "$BREW_TAP" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
    rm -rf "$(brew --cache)"/downloads/*intent* 2>/dev/null || true
    brew tap "$BREW_TAP" >/dev/null 2>&1 || die "could not tap $BREW_TAP"
    brew install "$BREW_TAP/intent" >/dev/null 2>&1 ||
      die "brew install failed -- and note it EXITS 0 on the defect this exists to catch, so a failure here is something else"
  fi

  step "the installed keg"
  # Every capture below is `|| true`-guarded or tested. THE FILE IS
  # `set -euo pipefail` (:60), so a bare assignment from a failing command
  # ABORTS THE RUN -- and this function's arms are SUPPOSED to fail. Driven
  # before it was noticed: the unknown-hook arm returned 1, set -e killed the
  # run between the last passing arm and the verdict, and `int macos smoke`
  # exited 1 having printed no failure list at all. A check that dies on its own
  # failing arm reports exactly like one that passed everything before it.
  keg=""
  if [ -n "$keg_override" ]; then
    keg="$keg_override"
    printf '    %-46s %s\n' "SUBJECT OVERRIDDEN" "not the installed keg"
  else
    keg="$(brew --cellar intent 2>/dev/null || true)/$(brew list --versions intent 2>/dev/null | awk '{print $2}' || true)"
  fi
  [ -d "$keg" ] || die "no installed keg for intent -- 'brew install $BREW_TAP/intent' first, or pass --reinstall"
  libexec="$keg/libexec"
  bin_intent="$keg/bin/intent"
  bin_daemon="$keg/bin/intentd"
  printf '    %-46s %s\n' "keg" "$keg"

  # ARM 1 -- THE DEFECT THIS COMMAND EXISTS FOR. Read `-x` back off the files
  # rather than trusting the formula's chmod: the formula is regenerated every
  # cut and the mode is lost in the network hop, so the only honest subject is
  # the installed file.
  step "the executable bit, read back off the installed files"
  for f in "$bin_intent" "$bin_daemon"; do
    if [ -x "$f" ]; then
      smoke_ok "$(basename "$f") is executable"
    else
      mode="$(stat -f '%OLp' "$f" 2>/dev/null || true)"
      smoke_fail "$f is mode ${mode:-unreadable} -- brew install succeeded and nothing in it runs" \
        "$(basename "$f") is executable"
    fi
  done

  # ARM 2 -- it runs at all, driven at the keg path, never through the symlink.
  step "the binaries answer"
  if out="$("$bin_intent" --version 2>&1)"; then
    smoke_ok "intent --version -> $out"
  else
    smoke_fail "\$KEG/bin/intent --version failed: $out" "intent --version"
  fi

  # ARM 3 -- THE CLASS, NOT THE INSTANCE (vc's ruling). Every path v3 resolves
  # against its install root must be present in the keg. Asserting only the
  # tree we happen to have just fixed is how the SECOND missing tree ships.
  step "every install-root path v3 resolves is present in the keg"
  for p in $SUPPORT_PATHS $INSTALL_BINARY_PATHS; do
    if [ -e "$libexec/$p" ]; then
      smoke_ok "$p"
    else
      smoke_fail "$libexec/$p is absent -- the keg cannot serve what the binary resolves" "$p"
    fi
  done

  # ARM 4 -- the rule library is not merely PRESENT but READABLE BY THE BINARY.
  # Presence and arming are different claims and 3.0.0 could have failed either
  # way; a directory that exists and yields zero rules gates nothing.
  step "the rule library arms"
  out="$("$bin_intent" claude rules list --lang shell 2>&1 || true)"
  case "$out" in
    *"total: 0 rule(s)"* | "")
      smoke_fail "'claude rules list --lang shell' on the keg reports: $(printf '%s' "${out:-<nothing>}" | grep 'total:' || printf '%s' "${out:-<nothing>}" | tail -1)" "shell rules arm" ;;
    *) smoke_ok "shell rules arm -- $(printf '%s' "$out" | tail -1)" ;;
  esac

  # ARM 5 -- INTENT_HOME lands on libexec, with a guard body under it. Driven
  # from a directory that is not a checkout, because resolving correctly while
  # standing inside the source tree proves nothing about a stranger's machine.
  step "INTENT_HOME resolves onto libexec, from outside any checkout"
  tmp="$(mktemp -d)"
  out="$( (cd "$tmp" && "$bin_intent" info 2>&1 || true) | awk '/INTENT_HOME/ { print $2 }' || true)"
  if [ "$out" = "$libexec" ]; then
    smoke_ok "INTENT_HOME -> libexec"
  else
    smoke_fail "INTENT_HOME resolved to '${out:-<empty>}', not $libexec" "INTENT_HOME -> libexec"
  fi
  if [ -f "$libexec/lib/templates/hooks/pre-commit-guards.sh" ]; then
    smoke_ok "pre-commit-guards.sh under INTENT_HOME"
  else
    smoke_fail "$libexec/lib/templates/hooks/pre-commit-guards.sh absent -- vendored estates fail open" \
      "pre-commit-guards.sh under INTENT_HOME"
  fi

  # ARM 6 -- the four hooks answer 0 and an unknown one answers 1, NEVER 2.
  # The names are the binary's, not Claude Code's lifecycle names: driving
  # SessionStart/UserPromptSubmit/Stop returns rc=1 and reads as a failing arm
  # when it is a wrong instrument (dc, measured 2026-08-26).
  step "the session hooks"
  for h in session-context require-in-session session-finish post-tool-advisory; do
    rc=0
    (cd "$tmp" && "$bin_intent" claude hook "$h" </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1) || rc=$?
    # `rc` captured on its own line rather than read inside the else branch:
    # `$?` there is the condition's status and survives only until the next
    # command, so a message that interpolates it is one edit from lying.
    if [ "$rc" -eq 0 ]; then
      smoke_ok "hook $h -> 0"
    else
      smoke_fail "hook $h returned $rc -- expected 0" "hook $h -> 0"
    fi
  done
  rc=0
  (cd "$tmp" && "$bin_intent" claude hook not-a-hook </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1) || rc=$?
  case "$rc" in
    1) smoke_ok "unknown hook -> 1" ;;
    2) smoke_fail "unknown hook returned 2, which the gate reads as 'fail open'" "unknown hook -> 1" ;;
    *) smoke_fail "unknown hook returned $rc, expected 1" "unknown hook -> 1" ;;
  esac
  rm -rf "$tmp"

  # ARM 7 -- A READ VERB LEAVES THE TREE ALONE. A REGRESSION GUARD, NOT A FIX.
  #
  # WHAT IT IS NOT. The claim that the shipped keg silently re-rendered tracked
  # views on a read was RETRACTED on 2026-08-26 after three independent
  # measurements refuted it -- the keg on two different clones, and the pair on
  # convergent and divergent fixtures. Nothing here is chasing a live defect.
  # It exists because a tree that quietly gains 167 modified files after a read
  # is invisible until someone runs `git status`, and the way that class returns
  # is a change nobody connects to views.
  #
  # THE ARM THAT MUST FALL SILENT NEEDS THE ARM THAT FIRES BESIDE IT. An
  # assertion that `git status` is EMPTY passes just as well when the verb never
  # ran, when the fixture has no tracked views, and when git is looking at the
  # wrong directory. So a WRITE verb runs immediately after against the SAME
  # fixture and must dirty it. Without that control this is a check that cannot
  # fail, which is the shape it is written to catch.
  #
  # AND THE READ VERB IS PROVEN TO HAVE RUN, NEVER TRUSTED ON rc. An
  # unparsed argument answers `unrecognized subcommand` at a perfectly plausible
  # exit code (dc, measured 2026-08-26: read as a product behaviour the product
  # never had). The arm asserts the thread id appears in the OUTPUT.
  #
  # THE STORE IS GITIGNORED BY HAND AND THAT IS ITSELF A FINDING. `init` writes
  # no .gitignore, so `git add -A` on a fresh project tracks intent/.cache/
  # intent.db -- and an untracked WAL beside a tracked db turns a routine
  # `git clean -fd` into silent data loss. Without the ignore, the db and its
  # WAL swamp this assertion and it reports dirt that says nothing about views.
  # `converge_gitignore` exists and only the migration path calls it; the fix is
  # to call it from `init`, and it is ic's file.
  #
  # HONEST LIMIT, STATED RATHER THAN DISCOVERED. The divergence planted below is
  # an INSERTED LINE, not an older RENDER FORMAT, and the fixture is built by
  # the same keg that reads it. So this proves the keg does not blindly rewrite
  # a view whose bytes it did not author. It does NOT prove that no divergence
  # shape can provoke a rewrite, and it is not the cross-version fixture, which
  # needs two kegs and does not fit inside a check whose subject is one.
  step "a read verb leaves tracked views alone, with a write verb as the control"
  tmp2="$(mktemp -d)"
  smoke_views_fixture "$bin_intent" "$tmp2"
  if [ -f "$tmp2/.fixture-ok" ]; then
    out="$( (cd "$tmp2" && "$bin_intent" st list --status all 2>&1) || true)"
    case "$out" in
      *ST0001*) smoke_ok "the read verb RAN (its output names ST0001)" ;;
      *) smoke_fail "'st list --status all' output does not name ST0001, so its exit code says nothing: ${out:-<empty>}" \
           "the read verb RAN" ;;
    esac

    dirt="$( (cd "$tmp2" && git status --porcelain) || true)"
    if [ -z "$dirt" ]; then
      smoke_ok "read verb left the tree clean"
    else
      smoke_fail "a read verb dirtied $(printf '%s\n' "$dirt" | grep -c .) path(s): $(printf '%s' "$dirt" | tr '\n' ' ')" \
        "read verb left the tree clean"
    fi

    if grep -q 'foreign line the store never rendered' "$tmp2/intent/st/steel_threads.md" 2>/dev/null; then
      smoke_ok "the planted divergence survived the read"
    else
      smoke_fail "the planted line is gone from steel_threads.md -- the view was re-rendered" \
        "the planted divergence survived the read"
    fi

    # THE CONTROL. It must FIRE. A green here is what entitles the three greens
    # above to mean anything at all.
    (cd "$tmp2" && "$bin_intent" st triage ST0001 >/dev/null 2>&1) || true
    dirt="$( (cd "$tmp2" && git status --porcelain) || true)"
    if [ -n "$dirt" ]; then
      smoke_ok "CONTROL: a write verb dirtied $(printf '%s\n' "$dirt" | grep -c .) path(s)"
    else
      smoke_fail "CONTROL FAILED: a write verb left the tree clean, so the fixture cannot show a write and the clean reads above prove nothing" \
        "CONTROL: a write verb dirties the tree"
    fi
  else
    smoke_fail "the views fixture could not be built, so nothing above it was measured -- an arm that cannot run is not an arm that passed" \
      "views fixture built"
  fi
  rm -rf "$tmp2"

  printf '\n'
  if [ -n "$SMOKE_FAILURES" ]; then
    die "the installed keg fails $SMOKE_FAIL_COUNT check(s):
$SMOKE_FAILURES"
  fi
  printf 'smoke: the installed keg at %s answers every arm.\n' "$keg"
  if [ "$reinstall" -eq 0 ]; then
    printf '  SCOPE: this asserted the keg ON DISK. It did not install it, so it cannot\n'
    printf '  distinguish a cold network install from a cache-preseeded one -- and the\n'
    printf '  preseed is what hid the mode-644 defect. Use --reinstall for that claim,\n'
    printf '  and expect it to unlink the binary other sessions are using.\n'
  fi
}

cmd_store_creds() {
  printf 'Storing Apple notary credentials in the login Keychain under "%s.*".\n' "$KC_PREFIX"
  printf 'Nothing is written to this repository. Values are not echoed.\n\n'
  printf 'APPLE_ID (Apple account email): '
  read -r a_id
  printf 'APPLE_APP_PASSWORD (app-specific password, not the account password): '
  read -rs a_pw
  printf '\nAPPLE_TEAM_ID (eg 76BQL8L47U): '
  read -r a_team
  printf '\n'
  for pair in "APPLE_ID:$a_id" "APPLE_APP_PASSWORD:$a_pw" "APPLE_TEAM_ID:$a_team"; do
    local k="${pair%%:*}" v="${pair#*:}"
    [ -n "$v" ] || die "$k was empty -- nothing stored"
    security add-generic-password -U -a "$USER" -s "$KC_PREFIX.$k" -w "$v" ||
      die "could not write $k to the keychain"
  done
  ok
  printf '  Stored. Verify with: int macos doctor\n'
}

SUBCOMMANDS="doctor stage sign notarize verify checksum prepare formula publish smoke env store-creds"

usage() {
  cat <<'USAGE'
intent macos <subcommand>

  THE PIPELINE, in order. Every step after `stage` acts on the staged copies in
  native/rust/target/dist -- never on target/release, which four sessions share
  and any `cargo build --release` will overwrite underneath you.

  prepare      RUN THE WHOLE SEQUENCE AS ONE PASS: stage, sign, notarize,
               checksum. This is the path for cutting a release; the individual
               steps below exist for diagnosis and for re-running one of them.

  stage        Copy the build output into target/dist under its shipping name
               (<binary>-<target-triple>), out of shared space. Wipes first.
  sign         Developer ID sign the STAGED artefacts, hardened runtime
  notarize     Submit the staged artefacts to Apple, wait, report
  checksum     Prove every staged artefact from a quarantined copy, and only
               then hash it -- REFUSES to hash anything unproven

  verify       Prove the staged artefacts without hashing them (read-only)
  formula      Emit the Homebrew formula to stdout. Generated, never hand-edited;
               refuses unless `checksum` has proven the artefacts it describes
  publish      Create the release, upload the artefacts, RE-DOWNLOAD them and
               confirm they hash to what we are about to publish, and only then
               push the formula to the tap. Refuses a dev version, refuses to
               overwrite an existing release, and requires the tag to exist
               already -- tagging is the release cut's job, not this one's.
               `--dry-run` runs every check and publishes nothing.
  smoke        Assert the INSTALLED KEG, which is the only subject that has been
               through the network hop. Reads -x back off both binaries, checks
               every install-root path v3 resolves is present, that the rule
               library ARMS rather than merely existing, and drives INTENT_HOME
               and the four hooks from outside any checkout. It also builds a
               throwaway project whose tracked view carries bytes the store did
               not render, and asserts a READ verb leaves it alone -- with a
               WRITE verb beside it that must dirty the tree, because an
               assertion that nothing happened passes on a broken fixture too.
               `--reinstall` does the cold tap install first -- it unlinks the
               binary other sessions are using, so it is opt-in.
  doctor       Preflight: identity present, a real test-sign, notary creds
  env          Print the resolved identity + which creds are present
  store-creds  Store APPLE_ID / APPLE_APP_PASSWORD / APPLE_TEAM_ID in the
               login Keychain (interactive; once per machine)
USAGE
}

case "${1:-}" in
  "" | -h | --help) usage; exit 0 ;;
  env) cmd_env ;;
  doctor) cmd_doctor ;;
  stage) cmd_stage ;;
  sign) cmd_sign ;;
  notarize | notarise) cmd_notarize ;;
  verify) cmd_verify ;;
  checksum) cmd_checksum ;;
  prepare) cmd_prepare ;;
  formula) shift; cmd_formula "$@" ;;
  publish) shift; cmd_publish "${1:-}" ;;
  smoke) shift; cmd_smoke "$@" ;;
  store-creds) cmd_store_creds ;;
  *) die "unknown macos subcommand: $1 (try: $SUBCOMMANDS)" ;;
esac
